WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.000
[suspenseful string music plays]

2
00:00:28.625 --> 00:00:30.142
[sighs]

3
00:00:30.166 --> 00:00:32.208
[fire crackling]

4
00:00:38.791 --> 00:00:40.625
[man] <i>Hard to know where to start.</i>

5
00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:45.226
<i>I guess to tell the story
of the Good Friday murder through my eyes,</i>

6
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:46.958
<i>I got to start here.</i>

7
00:00:47.625 --> 00:00:51.726
<i>Nine months ago, when this asshole deacon
said something way out of line,</i>

8
00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.017
<i>and I did this.</i>

9
00:00:53.041 --> 00:00:55.500
- [imperceptible]
- [grunts]

10
00:00:56.041 --> 00:00:57.208
- [thuds]
- [priest gasps]

11
00:00:58.333 --> 00:00:59.958
Oh, shit.

12
00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.083
So, you're a fighter, then?

13
00:01:03.583 --> 00:01:05.684
Uh, no, Father, absolutely not.

14
00:01:05.708 --> 00:01:09.017
Well, we have a deacon who would say
otherwise if his jaw wasn't broken.

15
00:01:09.041 --> 00:01:11.750
In my previous life, yes, I was a boxer,

16
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.809
and I lived on the streets.
I did some other things.

17
00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:18.708
We need fighters today,
but to fight the world,

18
00:01:19.458 --> 00:01:21.059
not ourselves.

19
00:01:21.083 --> 00:01:24.601
A priest is a shepherd.
The world is a wolf.

20
00:01:24.625 --> 00:01:25.833
No.

21
00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:33.601
[sighs softly] I... I don't believe that,
Father, respectfully.

22
00:01:33.625 --> 00:01:36.476
[hesitates] You start fighting wolves,
and before you know it,

23
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.291
everyone you don't understand is a wolf.

24
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:42.309
I still got that fighting instinct,
and I gave into it today,

25
00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:44.809
but Christ came to heal the world,
not fight it.

26
00:01:44.833 --> 00:01:46.184
I believe that.

27
00:01:46.208 --> 00:01:49.583
It's this, not this, you know?
[inhales deeply]

28
00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:52.666
I just want to be a good priest.

29
00:01:53.625 --> 00:01:56.892
Show broken people like me
the forgiveness and love of Christ.

30
00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:59.000
The world needs that so bad.

31
00:02:01.708 --> 00:02:04.583
You give me one more shot,
and I promise I'll do that.

32
00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:07.333
[church bell tolling in distance]

33
00:02:11.333 --> 00:02:14.000
- [church bell continues tolling]
- [vehicle horn honks]

34
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:16.851
[tolling stops]

35
00:02:16.875 --> 00:02:18.125
Your Excellency,

36
00:02:18.833 --> 00:02:21.017
you stuck your neck out for me
so many times.

37
00:02:21.041 --> 00:02:23.601
- I let you down, and whatever the--
- All right.

38
00:02:23.625 --> 00:02:24.791
Listen...

39
00:02:25.291 --> 00:02:28.226
Deacon Clark is famously a dick.

40
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.767
Nobody's actually that upset
that you clocked him.

41
00:02:30.791 --> 00:02:32.351
In fact, kind of the opposite.

42
00:02:32.375 --> 00:02:35.041
- But we do need to do something about it.
- [exhales]

43
00:02:36.041 --> 00:02:39.226
We're sending you
to a small parish in Chimney Rock.

44
00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:42.351
It's just one priest there now.

45
00:02:42.375 --> 00:02:43.559
[wind chime tinkling]

46
00:02:43.583 --> 00:02:44.976
Assistant pastor?

47
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.226
Yeah, well, curb your enthusiasm.

48
00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:48.250
What?

49
00:02:48.666 --> 00:02:53.392
[sighs] You're going
to Our Lady of Perpetual Fortitude,

50
00:02:53.416 --> 00:02:56.583
led by Monsignor Jefferson Wicks.
Have you heard of Jefferson Wicks?

51
00:02:57.458 --> 00:03:00.934
Okay, look, Wicks has his supporters here.
I'm not one of them.

52
00:03:00.958 --> 00:03:02.101
Between you and me,

53
00:03:02.125 --> 00:03:05.142
I think he's a few beads shy of
a full rosary and a real son of a bitch.

54
00:03:05.166 --> 00:03:06.059
[chuckles]

55
00:03:06.083 --> 00:03:10.833
But what's undeniable is that his flock
is shrinking and even calcifying.

56
00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:15.333
It could use some of what
you said in there. You understand?

57
00:03:15.958 --> 00:03:18.267
Not at all, but yes.

58
00:03:18.291 --> 00:03:20.434
Yes, yes, yes, yes, yes.

59
00:03:20.458 --> 00:03:23.392
- Spirit's got me. Let me at him.
- Hey. Hey, hey.

60
00:03:23.416 --> 00:03:27.041
This, not this, right? Mm?

61
00:03:31.166 --> 00:03:32.375
Good luck, kid.

62
00:03:33.041 --> 00:03:35.250
[intriguing music plays]

63
00:03:38.166 --> 00:03:40.791
[Jud] <i>So, that's how I came
to Chimney Rock.</i>

64
00:03:43.333 --> 00:03:45.142
<i>Daniel in the lion's den.</i>

65
00:03:45.166 --> 00:03:47.000
<i>David facing off Goliath.</i>

66
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.892
<i>Young, dumb, and full of Christ.</i>

67
00:03:49.916 --> 00:03:51.726
<i>Ready for anything.</i>

68
00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.184
[door creaks, closes]

69
00:03:54.208 --> 00:03:56.291
[intriguing music continues]

70
00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.291
[bag thuds]

71
00:04:04.083 --> 00:04:05.083
[music fades]

72
00:04:09.875 --> 00:04:12.291
[door creaking]

73
00:04:23.791 --> 00:04:24.875
Father Jefferson?

74
00:04:25.791 --> 00:04:28.059
Hello. Jud Duplenticy from Albany?

75
00:04:28.083 --> 00:04:30.875
Lord be with you,
Jud Duplenticy from Albany.

76
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.125
You here to take my church away from me?

77
00:04:37.458 --> 00:04:39.250
[chuckling] Oh. No.

78
00:04:39.833 --> 00:04:40.833
Good.

79
00:04:41.583 --> 00:04:44.000
Okay, call me Monsignor Wicks.

80
00:04:44.916 --> 00:04:46.208
I see you've met Martha.

81
00:04:47.083 --> 00:04:48.309
Martha? No.

82
00:04:48.333 --> 00:04:49.892
- Monsignor Wicks.
- Jesus!

83
00:04:49.916 --> 00:04:52.476
- I woke up early to polish the silver.
- [sighs]

84
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:53.851
It was a bit blotchy.

85
00:04:53.875 --> 00:04:55.476
That'll be fine, Martha.

86
00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.791
Father... Jud.

87
00:04:59.083 --> 00:05:00.375
You are welcome here.

88
00:05:00.875 --> 00:05:02.476
- Thank you, Martha.
- Mm.

89
00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:05.184
I was actually
just saying to Father Wicks...

90
00:05:05.208 --> 00:05:06.875
Monsignor Wicks.

91
00:05:07.708 --> 00:05:09.583
Monsignor, right. Sorry. [chuckles]

92
00:05:10.083 --> 00:05:11.708
And sorry for saying "Jesus".

93
00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.642
Hoo. [chuckles]
This is going great, I think.

94
00:05:15.666 --> 00:05:18.291
- Bishop Langstrom sent you, huh?
- Mm-hmm.

95
00:05:19.166 --> 00:05:20.250
Langstrom.

96
00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:22.392
- I know him well.
- [chuckles]

97
00:05:22.416 --> 00:05:24.708
He handpicked you, sent you here.

98
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.000
It says something to me.

99
00:05:29.583 --> 00:05:30.791
Tells me a lot.

100
00:05:31.916 --> 00:05:34.708
Well, I... I know
you're used to flying solo, but, um...

101
00:05:35.291 --> 00:05:36.916
I'm... I'm here to serve.

102
00:05:38.416 --> 00:05:39.625
Take my confession.

103
00:05:41.291 --> 00:05:43.083
[Wicks groans] All right.

104
00:05:43.791 --> 00:05:45.083
- Mm.
- [chuckles]

105
00:05:47.166 --> 00:05:49.684
Bless me, Father, for I have sinned.

106
00:05:49.708 --> 00:05:51.000
It's been, uh,

107
00:05:51.875 --> 00:05:54.291
six weeks since my last confession.

108
00:05:55.041 --> 00:05:57.083
[inhales deeply, sighs]

109
00:05:58.708 --> 00:06:01.250
I've envied the material wealth of others.

110
00:06:02.125 --> 00:06:06.750
I saw this luxury car commercial.
Sam had it on his TV. Lexus.

111
00:06:07.416 --> 00:06:08.625
And I thought,

112
00:06:09.125 --> 00:06:11.851
- "Ah! That is a good-looking car."
- [both chuckle]

113
00:06:11.875 --> 00:06:13.458
The coupe.

114
00:06:14.583 --> 00:06:17.958
Uh... I've envied the power of great men.

115
00:06:19.375 --> 00:06:22.726
Envied my grandfather's power as a priest.
I wanted that.

116
00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:24.041
Always did.

117
00:06:25.791 --> 00:06:26.791
Uh...

118
00:06:27.541 --> 00:06:28.958
I've masturbated,

119
00:06:30.166 --> 00:06:33.684
um, four times this week.

120
00:06:33.708 --> 00:06:36.601
Four or five, generally, in...
What'd I say, six weeks?

121
00:06:36.625 --> 00:06:39.791
Well, let's say 30 times, masturbated.

122
00:06:40.833 --> 00:06:44.458
Uh, this week, in my bed,
in the morning once.

123
00:06:44.958 --> 00:06:49.017
Once in the shower, standing up,
which was convenient.

124
00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:51.226
Just use a bath gel.

125
00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:52.226
[inhales deeply]

126
00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:55.517
Oh, once in the middle of the night,
after a dream about--

127
00:06:55.541 --> 00:06:58.184
- That's all right.
- About one of those Japanese cat cafés.

128
00:06:58.208 --> 00:07:00.601
- Okay.
- You know, I read this article,

129
00:07:00.625 --> 00:07:05.916
but the cats were girls,
and, you know, so...

130
00:07:06.583 --> 00:07:07.976
And I hadn't prepared,

131
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.125
so I had to finish into a copy
of <i>Catholic Chronicle</i> magazine,

132
00:07:12.833 --> 00:07:16.916
just what was on the end table,
which is probably its own kind of sin.

133
00:07:17.416 --> 00:07:20.416
I don't know, maybe not, but...
Not good. [chuckles]

134
00:07:21.541 --> 00:07:23.809
[Jud] <i>At the time,
I thought this was just weird.</i>

135
00:07:23.833 --> 00:07:24.726
[imperceptible]

136
00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:26.892
<i>But looking back now, I know.</i>

137
00:07:26.916 --> 00:07:29.309
<i>This was Wicks' first punch.</i>

138
00:07:29.333 --> 00:07:31.458
Five Hail Marys and one Glory Be.

139
00:07:33.166 --> 00:07:34.458
Thank you, Father.

140
00:07:40.041 --> 00:07:41.517
[Jud] <i>It wouldn't be his last.</i>

141
00:07:41.541 --> 00:07:42.875
[Wicks] Oh, and welcome

142
00:07:43.666 --> 00:07:45.083
to <i>my</i> church.

143
00:07:45.916 --> 00:07:48.642
- [door opens, closes]
- [sighs]

144
00:07:48.666 --> 00:07:51.184
<i>Over the next few weeks, I settled in</i>

145
00:07:51.208 --> 00:07:53.500
<i>at Our Lady of Perpetual Fortitude.</i>

146
00:07:54.333 --> 00:07:56.666
[intriguing music plays]

147
00:07:59.166 --> 00:08:03.791
<i>The only other full-time employee
was the groundskeeper, Samson.</i>

148
00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:05.583
<i>Sam.</i>

149
00:08:11.125 --> 00:08:12.809
[Sam] <i>It's Monsignor Wicks</i>

150
00:08:12.833 --> 00:08:17.000
who gives me the strength every day
to not go back to the bottle.

151
00:08:17.916 --> 00:08:19.017
He used to drink too.

152
00:08:19.041 --> 00:08:20.017
[music fades]

153
00:08:20.041 --> 00:08:24.083
But he said to me once,
"If I can stave off that demon, you can."

154
00:08:25.375 --> 00:08:27.291
And every day, it is a struggle.

155
00:08:28.166 --> 00:08:29.250
But I have.

156
00:08:30.541 --> 00:08:34.017
I credit him and... my sweet Martha.

157
00:08:34.041 --> 00:08:36.601
Your... your sweet Martha?

158
00:08:36.625 --> 00:08:38.333
I'd do anything for her.

159
00:08:39.708 --> 00:08:42.059
- My angel on Earth.
- [organ music playing]

160
00:08:42.083 --> 00:08:45.434
[Jud] <i>As far as the church goes,
Martha does it all.</i>

161
00:08:45.458 --> 00:08:48.726
<i>She keeps the books,
manages donations, files everything.</i>

162
00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:49.958
File that.

163
00:08:50.541 --> 00:08:52.934
<i>She launders the vestments,
stocks the supplies,</i>

164
00:08:52.958 --> 00:08:55.166
<i>feeds Wicks, plays the organ.</i>

165
00:08:58.875 --> 00:09:00.791
<i>She knew where the bodies were buried.</i>

166
00:09:01.583 --> 00:09:03.642
- So this is the crypt?
- [Sam] Yep.

167
00:09:03.666 --> 00:09:06.708
It's a damn shame.
Gotta put up a security camera.

168
00:09:07.208 --> 00:09:09.333
- [Jud] Is there an entrance?
- There is.

169
00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:11.708
This right here,

170
00:09:12.208 --> 00:09:13.333
it's a Lazarus door.

171
00:09:14.041 --> 00:09:16.892
Takes construction equipment
to open from the outside.

172
00:09:16.916 --> 00:09:19.875
But cantilevered as such that one push

173
00:09:20.458 --> 00:09:22.875
will send it tumbling
to the ground from inside.

174
00:09:24.125 --> 00:09:25.434
Who's in there then?

175
00:09:25.458 --> 00:09:26.541
- Prentice.
- [gasps]

176
00:09:27.166 --> 00:09:30.625
Wicks' grandfather,
the founder of this church.

177
00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.000
Was like a father to me.

178
00:09:33.666 --> 00:09:35.642
Makes me sick.

179
00:09:35.666 --> 00:09:40.000
These kids painting rocket ships
all over his sacred resting place.

180
00:09:46.708 --> 00:09:49.416
- [Wicks] What are you doing?
- Oh, uh...

181
00:09:50.208 --> 00:09:52.101
Well, I do a little wood-working,

182
00:09:52.125 --> 00:09:55.684
so thought I could borrow
some of Sam's tools, make a proper--

183
00:09:55.708 --> 00:09:56.976
No, we leave that.

184
00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.958
A reminder of the shameful sin
of the harlot whore.

185
00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:02.916
Take my confession.

186
00:10:04.875 --> 00:10:06.625
[Martha] Yes, the harlot whore.

187
00:10:08.125 --> 00:10:09.601
That was Wicks' mother.

188
00:10:09.625 --> 00:10:12.309
Okay, so what's the deal with that?

189
00:10:12.333 --> 00:10:14.625
She was a harlot and a whore.

190
00:10:15.458 --> 00:10:16.267
Okay.

191
00:10:16.291 --> 00:10:19.559
When Prentice entered the priesthood
and founded this church,

192
00:10:19.583 --> 00:10:21.833
he was widowed with a daughter.

193
00:10:22.958 --> 00:10:24.541
Grace was her name.

194
00:10:25.125 --> 00:10:26.726
Always a bad seed.

195
00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:32.142
She loved revealing clothes
and her fancy brands.

196
00:10:32.166 --> 00:10:33.851
Fancy brands... Oof.

197
00:10:33.875 --> 00:10:35.017
- Mm.
- Yeah.

198
00:10:35.041 --> 00:10:37.934
As a teenager, she slutted around bars,

199
00:10:37.958 --> 00:10:40.226
was soon pregnant by a drifter.

200
00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:41.142
[imperceptible]

201
00:10:41.166 --> 00:10:45.267
[Martha] <i>Prentice had
a vast family fortune in the bank.</i>

202
00:10:45.291 --> 00:10:48.392
- [intriguing music plays]
<i>- To protect his grandson, Wicks,</i>

203
00:10:48.416 --> 00:10:53.083
<i>he promised that
if Grace stayed under his roof,</i>

204
00:10:53.791 --> 00:10:56.916
the fortune would be willed to her.

205
00:10:57.916 --> 00:11:01.750
<i>And so the whore
waited for her father to die.</i>

206
00:11:02.666 --> 00:11:05.059
<i>Oh, it weighed on him heavily.</i>

207
00:11:05.083 --> 00:11:06.666
Martha, remember this.

208
00:11:07.833 --> 00:11:10.666
Wealth and the power that comes with it,

209
00:11:11.416 --> 00:11:12.666
is Eve's Apple.

210
00:11:13.916 --> 00:11:16.041
Temptation that leads to the fall.

211
00:11:17.291 --> 00:11:21.125
I must protect our loved ones
from its corrupting influence,

212
00:11:22.208 --> 00:11:23.625
at all costs.

213
00:11:24.375 --> 00:11:26.184
<i>His day came at last.</i>

214
00:11:26.208 --> 00:11:27.541
I was there.

215
00:11:28.625 --> 00:11:31.809
<i>I saw Prentice take his final communion</i>

216
00:11:31.833 --> 00:11:34.642
<i>and die on the holy altar.</i>

217
00:11:34.666 --> 00:11:36.166
<i>At peace.</i>

218
00:11:36.958 --> 00:11:40.458
The harlot whore
went straight to Prentice's attorney.

219
00:11:41.041 --> 00:11:44.125
"Give me my money," she said.

220
00:11:45.375 --> 00:11:46.833
And do you know what he said?

221
00:11:47.958 --> 00:11:52.458
"Yes, you are heir
to every single penny Prentice had."

222
00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:54.875
And in his accounts,

223
00:11:56.166 --> 00:11:59.166
not one hot dime.

224
00:11:59.875 --> 00:12:02.017
Mm, what did he do with the fortune?

225
00:12:02.041 --> 00:12:04.041
Gave it to the poor, say some.

226
00:12:04.666 --> 00:12:07.541
Threw it in the ocean, say others.
No one knows.

227
00:12:08.416 --> 00:12:09.416
It was gone.

228
00:12:10.375 --> 00:12:14.208
That holy man's final act of grace

229
00:12:15.333 --> 00:12:19.625
was to keep the corrupting evil
out of wicked hands.

230
00:12:20.666 --> 00:12:22.500
All that he left her...

231
00:12:25.541 --> 00:12:26.541
{\an8}was this.

232
00:12:27.166 --> 00:12:29.208
{\an8}"What is this?" she said.

233
00:12:30.291 --> 00:12:31.708
But I knew.

234
00:12:32.416 --> 00:12:34.250
Look not for Eve's Apple.

235
00:12:34.916 --> 00:12:38.309
Your inheritance is now Christ.

236
00:12:38.333 --> 00:12:40.583
[ominous music swells]

237
00:12:44.208 --> 00:12:46.833
[Martha] <i>That night, she had her revenge.</i>

238
00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:51.976
<i>In a demonic rage,</i>

239
00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:56.142
<i>- she defiled this holy place.</i>
- [grunts]

240
00:12:56.166 --> 00:12:58.601
[sinister music plays]

241
00:12:58.625 --> 00:13:00.059
[grunting]

242
00:13:00.083 --> 00:13:01.083
[screams]

243
00:13:01.916 --> 00:13:03.791
[grunts, yells]

244
00:13:07.041 --> 00:13:08.041
[grunts, yells]

245
00:13:11.416 --> 00:13:13.833
[Martha] <i>Blasphemy, desecration.</i>

246
00:13:15.416 --> 00:13:18.392
<i>- Evil incarnate.</i>
- [yells]

247
00:13:18.416 --> 00:13:19.708
[music fades]

248
00:13:21.250 --> 00:13:23.291
[Grace breathing heavily]

249
00:13:27.458 --> 00:13:28.458
[squeals]

250
00:13:29.166 --> 00:13:30.166
[grunts]

251
00:13:34.416 --> 00:13:35.458
[breath trembling]

252
00:13:37.208 --> 00:13:39.226
[low unsettling music plays]

253
00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:41.333
[breathing heavily]

254
00:13:43.291 --> 00:13:44.291
[sniffles]

255
00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:49.250
[thunder rumbling]

256
00:13:54.083 --> 00:13:56.434
[Martha] <i>I said, "Sister Grace,</i>

257
00:13:56.458 --> 00:14:01.458
<i>God, your Father,
will forgive you in His love."</i>

258
00:14:05.083 --> 00:14:06.083
[music fades]

259
00:14:07.541 --> 00:14:09.476
- [Martha screams]
- [yells]

260
00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:11.309
- [grunting]
- [yelping]

261
00:14:11.333 --> 00:14:13.416
[menacing music plays]

262
00:14:16.541 --> 00:14:17.934
[yelling]

263
00:14:17.958 --> 00:14:19.541
- [music stops]
- She died,

264
00:14:20.083 --> 00:14:22.351
throwing herself against Prentice's tomb.

265
00:14:22.375 --> 00:14:24.125
[screams]

266
00:14:25.541 --> 00:14:27.541
[Martha] <i>Brain aneurysm, they said.</i>

267
00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:30.666
Struck down, I say,

268
00:14:31.541 --> 00:14:34.875
by God in His mercy.

269
00:14:35.958 --> 00:14:37.500
Holy shit.

270
00:14:39.666 --> 00:14:40.666
Sorry, sorry.

271
00:14:41.666 --> 00:14:42.892
[intriguing music plays]

272
00:14:42.916 --> 00:14:46.309
[Jud] <i>The darkness of that story
was the bedrock of this place.</i>

273
00:14:46.333 --> 00:14:48.642
- [Wicks speaks indistinctly]
- [Jud] <i>You could feel it.</i>

274
00:14:48.666 --> 00:14:50.059
And he asked, "Who told you..."

275
00:14:50.083 --> 00:14:54.500
[Jud] <i>So what would draw someone
to make this their spiritual home?</i>

276
00:14:55.083 --> 00:14:58.059
<i>The core group of regulars
all had their reasons.</i>

277
00:14:58.083 --> 00:15:00.101
<i>And these will be our suspects.</i>

278
00:15:00.125 --> 00:15:01.833
<i>So I should introduce them.</i>

279
00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:06.184
{\an8}<i>Vera Draven, local attorney.
She was loyal and devoted.</i>

280
00:15:06.208 --> 00:15:07.666
{\an8}- [lock chirps]
- [whistle blows]

281
00:15:08.625 --> 00:15:11.934
[Vera] My dad was
Wicks' attorney and drinking buddy.

282
00:15:11.958 --> 00:15:14.142
[chuckles] The boys.

283
00:15:14.166 --> 00:15:18.601
And so you became a lawyer so you could
take over his family practice?

284
00:15:18.625 --> 00:15:21.791
I became a lawyer because
I wanted to do great things in the world.

285
00:15:23.125 --> 00:15:25.958
- But this place is my dad's legacy.
- [spoon clatters]

286
00:15:26.708 --> 00:15:27.726
And he wanted me to...

287
00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:30.184
- Thank you.
- ...keep it going after he passed.

288
00:15:30.208 --> 00:15:32.684
So here I am.

289
00:15:32.708 --> 00:15:34.601
- Yo, yo. Hey, Vera.
- [door closes]

290
00:15:34.625 --> 00:15:37.017
- Hi. Oh. Thank you.
- Would you fill this up?

291
00:15:37.041 --> 00:15:39.601
[Jud] <i>Her adopted son, Cy,
just moved back home</i>

292
00:15:39.625 --> 00:15:42.392
<i>after trying and failing
to get a foothold in politics.</i>

293
00:15:42.416 --> 00:15:43.392
Hi.

294
00:15:43.416 --> 00:15:44.434
Hey.

295
00:15:44.458 --> 00:15:45.625
Okay.

296
00:15:46.666 --> 00:15:48.184
It's nice having him back?

297
00:15:48.208 --> 00:15:50.375
[intriguing music plays]

298
00:15:51.416 --> 00:15:54.392
[Jud] <i>When Vera was young,
just out of law school,</i>

299
00:15:54.416 --> 00:15:58.226
<i>her dad came home
with ten-year-old Cy, out of the blue,</i>

300
00:15:58.250 --> 00:16:00.642
<i>and told Vera she was gonna raise him.</i>

301
00:16:00.666 --> 00:16:02.166
<i>No questions asked.</i>

302
00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:07.416
<i>The whole town knew he was
obviously her illegitimate brother.</i>

303
00:16:08.083 --> 00:16:09.500
<i>But Vera accepted it.</i>

304
00:16:10.458 --> 00:16:11.726
<i>She did it.</i>

305
00:16:11.750 --> 00:16:14.392
I've given up a lot to... [inhales deeply]

306
00:16:14.416 --> 00:16:19.041
...be loyal to my dad
and to Cy and to Wicks.

307
00:16:20.375 --> 00:16:21.684
So I think that

308
00:16:21.708 --> 00:16:25.184
when my dad
is looking down on me from heaven,

309
00:16:25.208 --> 00:16:28.684
I think he is very... [inhales deeply]

310
00:16:28.708 --> 00:16:30.500
...pleased with me.

311
00:16:32.875 --> 00:16:34.291
So I guess that's nice.

312
00:16:34.958 --> 00:16:36.017
[Cy] <i>I came this close.</i>

313
00:16:36.041 --> 00:16:38.642
I was the GOP golden boy, the great hope.

314
00:16:38.666 --> 00:16:41.267
I've got connections and ins and outs.

315
00:16:41.291 --> 00:16:43.476
I was on the cusp,
but I just couldn't engage voters.

316
00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:47.476
I didn't... [inhales] have that
cult of personality thing, I guess.

317
00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:50.392
It's hard connecting with people
in a genuine way.

318
00:16:50.416 --> 00:16:52.184
- I know.
- [Jud] Mm-hmm.

319
00:16:52.208 --> 00:16:54.684
I tried everything. Believe me,
I hammered the race thing.

320
00:16:54.708 --> 00:16:56.976
I hammered the gender thing,
the trans thing,

321
00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.184
the border thing, the homeless thing,

322
00:16:59.208 --> 00:17:03.125
the war thing, the election thing,
the abortion thing, the climate thing.

323
00:17:03.916 --> 00:17:07.892
Thing about induction stoves,
Israel, library books, vaccines,

324
00:17:07.916 --> 00:17:11.059
pronouns, AK-47s, socialism, BLM,

325
00:17:11.083 --> 00:17:15.000
CRT, the CDC, DEI, 5G, everything.

326
00:17:15.708 --> 00:17:19.708
All of it I did.
Nobody, just nothing. [sighs]

327
00:17:20.458 --> 00:17:22.642
People are just numb these days.
I don't know why.

328
00:17:22.666 --> 00:17:25.601
[hesitates] Maybe we need to get back
to fundamentals, you know,

329
00:17:25.625 --> 00:17:29.142
basic building blocks
on how to genuinely inspire people.

330
00:17:29.166 --> 00:17:31.267
The basics,
like, show them something they hate

331
00:17:31.291 --> 00:17:34.017
and then make them afraid it's gonna
take away something they love?

332
00:17:34.041 --> 00:17:35.726
[soft intriguing music plays]

333
00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:37.101
[hesitates] Well, no.

334
00:17:37.125 --> 00:17:38.517
- [indistinct chatter]
- Vera.

335
00:17:38.541 --> 00:17:41.309
- [Jud] <i>Nat Sharp, the town's local doctor.</i>
- [imperceptible]

336
00:17:41.333 --> 00:17:43.625
<i>His life revolved around his wife, Darla.</i>

337
00:17:44.291 --> 00:17:45.934
<i>She was his everything.</i>

338
00:17:45.958 --> 00:17:47.851
- [music stops]
- Darla left me last week.

339
00:17:47.875 --> 00:17:49.625
[tuts] Oh, I'm sorry.

340
00:17:50.125 --> 00:17:53.000
Took the kids, moved to Tucson

341
00:17:54.166 --> 00:17:56.791
with some guy that she met
on a Phish message board.

342
00:17:58.791 --> 00:17:59.791
[smacks lips]

343
00:18:00.416 --> 00:18:01.601
Phish the band?

344
00:18:01.625 --> 00:18:03.125
I had no idea.

345
00:18:03.958 --> 00:18:06.851
- [somber music plays]
- [Jud] <i>Dr. Nat was spinning out.</i>

346
00:18:06.875 --> 00:18:10.767
<i>He wasn't successful enough,
rich enough, good enough for her.</i>

347
00:18:10.791 --> 00:18:13.000
<i>He would do anything to get her back.</i>

348
00:18:15.875 --> 00:18:17.101
[intriguing music plays]

349
00:18:17.125 --> 00:18:19.809
<i>The closest thing we have
to a local celebrity,</i>

350
00:18:19.833 --> 00:18:22.041
<i>the sci-fi writer Lee Ross.</i>

351
00:18:23.125 --> 00:18:25.041
<i>You probably know
at least some of his books.</i>

352
00:18:25.666 --> 00:18:28.059
{\an8}The Crescent Limbo <i>series,</i>
Ice Pick of Time,

353
00:18:28.083 --> 00:18:29.500
{\an8}The Crystaline Juncture.

354
00:18:30.291 --> 00:18:32.767
<i>Ten years ago,
Lee moved here from New York,</i>

355
00:18:32.791 --> 00:18:35.851
<i>connected with Wicks and the church,
and as he puts it...</i>

356
00:18:35.875 --> 00:18:37.434
Unplugged my brain, you know,

357
00:18:37.458 --> 00:18:41.958
from the... [inhales] liberal hive mind
and... [exhales] come here and...

358
00:18:42.875 --> 00:18:46.833
[Jud] <i>His book sales and popular standing
have been in slow decline ever since.</i>

359
00:18:47.958 --> 00:18:51.351
<i>But he'd spent the last year
writing a massive book about Wicks.</i>

360
00:18:51.375 --> 00:18:52.934
His teachings, my reflections,

361
00:18:52.958 --> 00:18:56.434
essays and recollections
of an acolyte at the feet of a prophet.

362
00:18:56.458 --> 00:18:58.684
[Jud] The Holy Man and the Troubadour.

363
00:18:58.708 --> 00:19:02.392
<i>I found it a tough read,
but he pinned all of his hopes on it.</i>

364
00:19:02.416 --> 00:19:05.851
This is my last chance ticket
out of Substack hell.

365
00:19:05.875 --> 00:19:08.625
I can't take it anymore.
My readers these days...

366
00:19:09.125 --> 00:19:11.517
I mean, they are survivalist freaks.

367
00:19:11.541 --> 00:19:14.226
They all look like John Goodman
in <i>The Big Lebowski.</i>

368
00:19:14.250 --> 00:19:15.583
[chuckles]

369
00:19:16.458 --> 00:19:18.101
[church bell tolling]

370
00:19:18.125 --> 00:19:21.291
This little shitwick, Cy.

371
00:19:22.041 --> 00:19:25.226
He's got his little influencer fangs
in the Monsignor lately.

372
00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:27.976
We don't like him. We're all like,
"Wicks. Be careful,

373
00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:32.166
you gotta shake him off.
He's bad news. Opportunistic poetaster."

374
00:19:33.166 --> 00:19:36.767
We're gonna have to protect Wicks
from these leeching millennials.

375
00:19:36.791 --> 00:19:39.642
[Jud] <i>Simone was new to town,
and new to the church.</i>

376
00:19:39.666 --> 00:19:43.291
<i>- She'd been a world-class cellist.</i>
- [playing dramatic, somber tune]

377
00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:49.559
<i>Forced to retire five years ago
for health reasons.</i>

378
00:19:49.583 --> 00:19:52.476
<i>Chronic pain. Some mysterious nerve thing</i>

379
00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:54.809
<i>- that doctors cannot diagnose.</i>
- Sorry. [panting]

380
00:19:54.833 --> 00:19:57.059
I believed they could heal me.

381
00:19:57.083 --> 00:19:58.791
[clicks tongue] Suckered.

382
00:19:59.291 --> 00:20:00.684
Dipshit <i>moi.</i>

383
00:20:00.708 --> 00:20:02.142
[breathes deeply]

384
00:20:02.166 --> 00:20:04.476
I mean, to take someone's faith

385
00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:06.208
and exploit it for money

386
00:20:08.083 --> 00:20:09.500
is the ultimate evil.

387
00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:13.142
Don't you think it is?

388
00:20:13.166 --> 00:20:15.375
Yeah. Yeah, it's bad.

389
00:20:16.791 --> 00:20:18.916
But I understand wanting to believe.

390
00:20:20.958 --> 00:20:22.559
This feels different, though.

391
00:20:22.583 --> 00:20:24.309
[intriguing music plays]

392
00:20:24.333 --> 00:20:26.833
Faith in God to heal me.

393
00:20:29.041 --> 00:20:30.250
This is different.

394
00:20:33.625 --> 00:20:36.250
I feel hopeful now.

395
00:20:38.125 --> 00:20:40.416
Like a miracle could happen.

396
00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:45.916
That's how Monsignor Wicks makes me feel.

397
00:20:49.750 --> 00:20:52.125
- [intriguing music continues]
- [wind gusting]

398
00:20:55.041 --> 00:20:56.684
[indistinct chatter]

399
00:20:56.708 --> 00:20:59.708
- The Spirit really moved him today, huh?
- [gasps]

400
00:21:00.958 --> 00:21:02.166
Monsignor!

401
00:21:03.083 --> 00:21:04.333
Ah, my warrior.

402
00:21:04.916 --> 00:21:07.976
- [Jud] <i>Wicks kept his core group tight.</i>
- [laughs]

403
00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:11.583
[Jud] <i>And the seductive power
of his charisma was undeniable.</i>

404
00:21:13.666 --> 00:21:14.875
<i>But his method...</i>

405
00:21:15.875 --> 00:21:17.892
<i>Every week, he would pick someone out,</i>

406
00:21:17.916 --> 00:21:20.934
<i>a newcomer usually, and he would attack.</i>

407
00:21:20.958 --> 00:21:24.125
The world wants us all to be okay.

408
00:21:25.958 --> 00:21:29.791
Any of your choices.
Make your choices. They're your choices.

409
00:21:30.291 --> 00:21:31.500
Don't feel bad.

410
00:21:32.208 --> 00:21:33.500
Have that affair.

411
00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:35.184
Tell that lie.

412
00:21:35.208 --> 00:21:37.601
Have that child out of wedlock.

413
00:21:37.625 --> 00:21:40.500
Satisfy your selfish heart.

414
00:21:41.125 --> 00:21:42.517
Selfish.

415
00:21:42.541 --> 00:21:43.666
Yes.

416
00:21:44.208 --> 00:21:47.333
Depriving that child of a family.

417
00:21:48.250 --> 00:21:49.583
Of a father.

418
00:21:50.083 --> 00:21:52.267
An assault on our castle.

419
00:21:52.291 --> 00:21:54.351
The institution of manhood.

420
00:21:54.375 --> 00:21:56.892
My own mother made
that selfish choice with me.

421
00:21:56.916 --> 00:21:59.101
And I curse her selfish heart for it.

422
00:21:59.125 --> 00:22:01.601
Every day of my life.

423
00:22:01.625 --> 00:22:05.851
Putting her needs and wants
before the family God intended.

424
00:22:05.875 --> 00:22:07.166
I am enough!

425
00:22:07.750 --> 00:22:10.392
- Me!
- [tense music plays]

426
00:22:10.416 --> 00:22:14.416
Selfish, harlot heart, you are not.

427
00:22:15.208 --> 00:22:16.684
Might as well beat that child.

428
00:22:16.708 --> 00:22:19.434
Yes, might as well starve that child.

429
00:22:19.458 --> 00:22:22.351
Defy the family that the Lord intended.

430
00:22:22.375 --> 00:22:26.291
And watch your child
burn beneath that burden.

431
00:22:27.208 --> 00:22:29.101
- [music subsides]
- [imperceptible]

432
00:22:29.125 --> 00:22:31.333
[Jud] <i>This is not the true church.</i>

433
00:22:32.291 --> 00:22:35.351
<i>You ask even the most hardcore
of those in the pews,</i>

434
00:22:35.375 --> 00:22:38.101
<i>they'll say, no,
of course this is not what they believe.</i>

435
00:22:38.125 --> 00:22:40.934
<i>It's Wicks being Wicks,
pushing it too far.</i>

436
00:22:40.958 --> 00:22:44.934
<i>And what he's pushing for
every time is a walkout.</i>

437
00:22:44.958 --> 00:22:46.351
[intriguing music plays]

438
00:22:46.375 --> 00:22:47.875
<i>Why does he do this?</i>

439
00:22:48.458 --> 00:22:51.458
<i>Because when that person walks out,
everyone watches.</i>

440
00:22:52.041 --> 00:22:54.767
<i>And even if in the light of day
it's indefensible,</i>

441
00:22:54.791 --> 00:22:58.017
<i>deep down in the dark,
it scratches an itch.</i>

442
00:22:58.041 --> 00:22:59.916
[imperceptible]

443
00:23:02.250 --> 00:23:05.666
[Jud] <i>And by staying put in that pew,
a side is taken.</i>

444
00:23:06.916 --> 00:23:08.601
<i>Wicks' side.</i>

445
00:23:08.625 --> 00:23:11.684
<i>Testing tolerances,
tapping deep poisoned wells,</i>

446
00:23:11.708 --> 00:23:13.767
<i>hardening, binding with complicity.</i>

447
00:23:13.791 --> 00:23:17.309
[man over radio] <i>...for a two-run homer
and that brings the Cubs...</i>

448
00:23:17.333 --> 00:23:18.916
[grunts] Mm.

449
00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:22.559
- [Jud] <i>So I try to offer a counterbalance.</i>
- [music fades]

450
00:23:22.583 --> 00:23:26.017
Okay, welcome to our first
Father Jud prayer group.

451
00:23:26.041 --> 00:23:28.583
Um, thank you all for being here.

452
00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:32.017
And this is all about breaking down walls

453
00:23:32.041 --> 00:23:34.541
between us and Christ,

454
00:23:35.250 --> 00:23:38.083
us and each other, us and the world.

455
00:23:39.333 --> 00:23:40.958
When I was 17,

456
00:23:41.458 --> 00:23:42.976
I was a boxer

457
00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:45.583
and I... I... killed a man in the ring.

458
00:23:47.458 --> 00:23:49.375
I built up so many walls of...

459
00:23:50.041 --> 00:23:52.833
of anger, addiction, violence.

460
00:23:54.333 --> 00:23:56.541
It was only when I felt safe enough to...

461
00:23:57.125 --> 00:24:00.934
to put my dukes down, open my arms,

462
00:24:00.958 --> 00:24:03.125
um, confess my deepest sin.

463
00:24:03.791 --> 00:24:06.208
That was the day
that Christ saved my life.

464
00:24:06.958 --> 00:24:09.559
It... He didn't... He didn't transform me.

465
00:24:09.583 --> 00:24:12.351
He sustains me every day.

466
00:24:12.375 --> 00:24:13.851
[clock ticking]

467
00:24:13.875 --> 00:24:15.458
That's daily bread, right?

468
00:24:16.958 --> 00:24:19.083
I think that's what the church should be.

469
00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:22.416
That's what I want
this church to be, for me

470
00:24:23.083 --> 00:24:24.500
and for all of you.

471
00:24:29.166 --> 00:24:30.458
- [Vera chuckles softly]
- So...

472
00:24:33.625 --> 00:24:36.041
Monsignor Wicks isn't coming?

473
00:24:38.041 --> 00:24:39.666
[hesitates] No, but...

474
00:24:40.458 --> 00:24:41.434
No.

475
00:24:41.458 --> 00:24:44.601
So, I thought we could just talk
and start sharing and--

476
00:24:44.625 --> 00:24:46.309
But he knows that we're doing these?

477
00:24:46.333 --> 00:24:48.559
Sure, of course. Of course, I'll tell him.

478
00:24:48.583 --> 00:24:50.809
I just... I wanted us to connect and, uh...

479
00:24:50.833 --> 00:24:53.101
You'll tell him? You'll... You'll tell him?

480
00:24:53.125 --> 00:24:56.625
Contraction of the simple future tense
meaning you haven't yet

481
00:24:57.250 --> 00:24:58.458
told him?

482
00:24:59.416 --> 00:25:00.809
Why wouldn't you tell him?

483
00:25:00.833 --> 00:25:02.875
- This feels kind of weird.
- [Jud] Okay, guys.

484
00:25:03.458 --> 00:25:06.517
[chuckles] This is a prayer meeting.
It's... it's not a secret or anything.

485
00:25:06.541 --> 00:25:08.767
- Well, it's a secret prayer meeting.
- No.

486
00:25:08.791 --> 00:25:10.559
That's literally what it is.

487
00:25:10.583 --> 00:25:11.809
I just texted the Monsignor.

488
00:25:11.833 --> 00:25:14.309
Great, so... [chuckles]
it isn't a secret anymore.

489
00:25:14.333 --> 00:25:17.601
Now, if we could get back to breaking
down those walls through Christ's love--

490
00:25:17.625 --> 00:25:18.642
[cell phone chimes]

491
00:25:18.666 --> 00:25:21.142
- He says, "What the holy heck?"
- Oh.

492
00:25:21.166 --> 00:25:23.434
- Jeez.
- [Vera] I'm... I'm sorry. Father,

493
00:25:23.458 --> 00:25:27.517
I came because I thought
this was an official church function.

494
00:25:27.541 --> 00:25:29.809
Only he didn't use the word "heck".

495
00:25:29.833 --> 00:25:31.434
- [laughs]
- Okay, thank you, Martha.

496
00:25:31.458 --> 00:25:32.851
This is a church function, Vera.

497
00:25:32.875 --> 00:25:34.767
It's at a church.
It's with me. It's official.

498
00:25:34.791 --> 00:25:36.142
- Um...
- Or "holy".

499
00:25:36.166 --> 00:25:38.434
- Show... Can I see that?
- I think I should go.

500
00:25:38.458 --> 00:25:39.517
- [Nat] Okay.
- [Lee] Bye.

501
00:25:39.541 --> 00:25:41.476
- [Vera] Thank you. Uh, Cyrus?
- Yeah.

502
00:25:41.500 --> 00:25:42.434
[Nat] Oh, wow.

503
00:25:42.458 --> 00:25:44.875
Yeah, sorry, Father.
I don't want to piss him off.

504
00:25:45.583 --> 00:25:47.059
Nice try, guy.

505
00:25:47.083 --> 00:25:48.101
Thanks, Doc.

506
00:25:48.125 --> 00:25:50.601
I'm probably gonna post this tomorrow.
Can I tag you?

507
00:25:50.625 --> 00:25:51.851
I'd prefer you didn't.

508
00:25:51.875 --> 00:25:53.017
I probably will anyway.

509
00:25:53.041 --> 00:25:54.166
- I know.
- [door opens]

510
00:25:55.000 --> 00:25:56.000
[door closes]

511
00:25:56.625 --> 00:26:00.351
I'm so sorry your little coup failed
this afternoon, Father.

512
00:26:00.375 --> 00:26:02.017
My coup? Martha, really?

513
00:26:02.041 --> 00:26:05.517
If we want to pray
or need to confess something,

514
00:26:05.541 --> 00:26:07.976
we can do it with Monsignor Wicks.

515
00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:10.892
Can you? 'Cause you all seem
scared to death of the guy.

516
00:26:10.916 --> 00:26:13.976
Could you walk into that church
of your own free will

517
00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:17.267
and confess your deepest sin to Wicks,
Martha, without fear?

518
00:26:17.291 --> 00:26:20.726
'Cause... 'cause if not,
this whole place is a whitewashed tomb.

519
00:26:20.750 --> 00:26:23.226
Yes, I could.

520
00:26:23.250 --> 00:26:24.416
Well,

521
00:26:25.625 --> 00:26:27.041
good!

522
00:26:30.041 --> 00:26:32.041
[church bell tolling]

523
00:26:40.666 --> 00:26:41.666
[scoffs]

524
00:26:48.458 --> 00:26:49.684
[groans]

525
00:26:49.708 --> 00:26:52.250
[panting]

526
00:26:56.916 --> 00:26:58.416
[exhales sharply]

527
00:26:59.583 --> 00:27:03.416
[Jud] <i>Holy Week, the week
of special services leading up to Easter.</i>

528
00:27:04.375 --> 00:27:07.184
{\an8}<i>It was on Palm Sunday
that I finally broke.</i>

529
00:27:07.208 --> 00:27:10.500
[Wicks] <i>And then, uh...
twice in the shower this week</i>

530
00:27:11.333 --> 00:27:14.791
doing that thing I told you about
where I hold my hand upside down?

531
00:27:16.041 --> 00:27:18.458
Five Our Fathers, five Hail Marys.

532
00:27:19.791 --> 00:27:22.250
Well, we're at nine months now, Jud.

533
00:27:23.791 --> 00:27:28.583
How are you enjoying it here?
Are you breaking down some... walls?

534
00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:36.125
Bless me, Father, for I have sinned.
It's been a week since my last confession.

535
00:27:38.916 --> 00:27:40.101
[inhales sharply]

536
00:27:40.125 --> 00:27:42.833
I betrayed a fellow priest's privacy.

537
00:27:44.041 --> 00:27:47.458
I know that Martha keeps her
medical bills filed in the office, so...

538
00:27:48.416 --> 00:27:49.625
I went through them.

539
00:27:50.666 --> 00:27:53.351
And I learned you had
a radical prostatectomy five years ago,

540
00:27:53.375 --> 00:27:56.708
making you physically incapable
of an erection.

541
00:27:59.166 --> 00:28:01.583
I can handle whatever this is.

542
00:28:02.125 --> 00:28:05.684
For the past nine months,
I've seen the way you tend this flock,

543
00:28:05.708 --> 00:28:07.101
and I don't like it.

544
00:28:07.125 --> 00:28:08.267
You don't like it?

545
00:28:08.291 --> 00:28:10.184
No, Monsignor, I don't.

546
00:28:10.208 --> 00:28:11.476
Nat Sharp.

547
00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:14.767
Man needs to forgive
and start living his life.

548
00:28:14.791 --> 00:28:17.767
And Christ's love should be
a launching pad for that, and instead,

549
00:28:17.791 --> 00:28:19.559
he's just, every day,

550
00:28:19.583 --> 00:28:22.851
getting more and more angry
and bitter against his ex-wife,

551
00:28:22.875 --> 00:28:23.958
<i>- against women.</i>
- [grunts]

552
00:28:24.916 --> 00:28:28.226
It's bad. And Lee, he's a storyteller.

553
00:28:28.250 --> 00:28:31.809
And it's like his superpower
has been turned against him.

554
00:28:31.833 --> 00:28:34.767
The only story he tells is,
"The world is out to get me."

555
00:28:34.791 --> 00:28:38.101
He was brilliant funny,
and smart and respected,

556
00:28:38.125 --> 00:28:43.767
and now he's just spinning out
and angry all the time, paranoid.

557
00:28:43.791 --> 00:28:46.916
Did you know he literally
built a moat around his house?

558
00:28:47.500 --> 00:28:48.500
Really?

559
00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:52.333
[Jud] <i>I mean,
it's mostly symbolic, but yeah.</i>

560
00:28:53.125 --> 00:28:55.101
And Simone, I'm sorry, Monsignor,

561
00:28:55.125 --> 00:28:57.392
but I'm afraid
you've taken advantage of her.

562
00:28:57.416 --> 00:28:59.333
I've seen the donation numbers.

563
00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:02.101
I know how much she's giving,
and the past few months,

564
00:29:02.125 --> 00:29:03.892
she's basically supporting this place.

565
00:29:03.916 --> 00:29:07.684
<i>And yes, I believe in the possibility
of miracles through Christ,</i>

566
00:29:07.708 --> 00:29:09.791
<i>but what you're giving her is not that.</i>

567
00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:13.184
It's transactional, through you,

568
00:29:13.208 --> 00:29:15.625
and God help you,
it makes her feel betrayed again.

569
00:29:16.208 --> 00:29:17.851
God help me. Anything else?

570
00:29:17.875 --> 00:29:20.184
Yeah, why not? Cy Draven.

571
00:29:20.208 --> 00:29:23.642
<i>Doesn't this new YouTube stuff
he's doing worry you?</i>

572
00:29:23.666 --> 00:29:26.142
{\an8}<i>Every week now, he takes clips
from what you say in Mass</i>

573
00:29:26.166 --> 00:29:30.726
{\an8}<i>and plugs them into his political rants.
He's co-opting, and honestly,</i>

574
00:29:30.750 --> 00:29:33.392
{\an8}<i>I think misrepresenting the church
in a dangerous way.</i>

575
00:29:33.416 --> 00:29:34.559
[sighs]

576
00:29:34.583 --> 00:29:38.333
When was the last time
a new person lasted more than one Sunday?

577
00:29:38.958 --> 00:29:40.333
Word's gotten out.

578
00:29:40.916 --> 00:29:42.892
Every week now, it's just this...

579
00:29:42.916 --> 00:29:44.976
this hardened cyst of regulars,

580
00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:49.375
and it seems like you're intentionally
keeping them angry and afraid.

581
00:29:50.333 --> 00:29:52.017
Is that how Christ led his flock?

582
00:29:52.041 --> 00:29:54.250
Is that what we're supposed to do?

583
00:29:59.250 --> 00:30:00.642
- [grunts]
- [groans]

584
00:30:00.666 --> 00:30:02.684
- [crows cawing, fluttering]
- [coughing]

585
00:30:02.708 --> 00:30:06.434
Right now, you're angry. You should be.

586
00:30:06.458 --> 00:30:08.851
It'd be dangerous if you weren't.
I'd see you're helpless,

587
00:30:08.875 --> 00:30:11.000
and I'd do it again and again.

588
00:30:11.791 --> 00:30:14.142
I'm the world. You're the church.

589
00:30:14.166 --> 00:30:17.125
- Stay there. Stay there. Stay.
- [groaning]

590
00:30:18.500 --> 00:30:21.958
- Aha. Good. Right. Yes.
- [pants, grunts]

591
00:30:22.541 --> 00:30:26.625
Anger. Anger lets us fight back,
take back the ground we've lost.

592
00:30:27.208 --> 00:30:29.375
And we've lost so much ground.

593
00:30:29.958 --> 00:30:31.059
And now you're afraid.

594
00:30:31.083 --> 00:30:34.041
Look at those bare-knuckle instincts
coming back. Good!

595
00:30:34.875 --> 00:30:37.309
You're afraid I'm gonna come at you again.

596
00:30:37.333 --> 00:30:40.750
- But you're protecting yourself.
- [pants, exhales]

597
00:30:43.125 --> 00:30:46.541
Because the world wants to destroy us.

598
00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:51.309
Your version of love
and forgiveness is a sop.

599
00:30:51.333 --> 00:30:54.392
It's going along
to get along with modernity,

600
00:30:54.416 --> 00:30:56.684
not wanting to offend this garbage world.

601
00:30:56.708 --> 00:31:00.041
Meanwhile, they destroy us.

602
00:31:00.708 --> 00:31:05.125
Feminist Marxist whores.
Bit by bit, they do.

603
00:31:07.208 --> 00:31:10.416
But I carry my burden. I hold the line.

604
00:31:12.458 --> 00:31:13.541
And you?

605
00:31:14.750 --> 00:31:17.166
You simpering child from Albany?

606
00:31:19.666 --> 00:31:21.291
Are you gonna get angry

607
00:31:22.375 --> 00:31:23.750
and fight?

608
00:31:32.083 --> 00:31:34.083
You're poisoning this church.

609
00:31:35.250 --> 00:31:37.666
And I'll do whatever it takes to save it.

610
00:31:39.458 --> 00:31:41.291
To cut you out like a cancer.

611
00:31:44.500 --> 00:31:46.916
Five Our Fathers, five Hail Marys.

612
00:32:02.666 --> 00:32:03.666
[door opens]

613
00:32:07.583 --> 00:32:09.583
[Jud] <i>Christ, you didn't give up on me.</i>

614
00:32:10.166 --> 00:32:12.392
<i>I'm not giving up on this church.</i>

615
00:32:12.416 --> 00:32:14.809
[thunder rumbling]

616
00:32:14.833 --> 00:32:16.833
<i>But he was one step ahead of me.</i>

617
00:32:17.666 --> 00:32:20.083
[Wicks] Father Jud's prayer meeting?

618
00:32:20.791 --> 00:32:22.267
I have kept this church.

619
00:32:22.291 --> 00:32:24.059
I have fortified it with the truth of God.

620
00:32:24.083 --> 00:32:27.434
And now the betrayal to find my authority

621
00:32:27.458 --> 00:32:30.642
and faith and life itself challenged,

622
00:32:30.666 --> 00:32:32.833
and from inside my own sanctuary.

623
00:32:35.333 --> 00:32:36.416
Get out!

624
00:32:37.875 --> 00:32:38.684
[door slams]

625
00:32:38.708 --> 00:32:40.059
[breathing heavily]

626
00:32:40.083 --> 00:32:41.767
[Jud] <i>Wicks' final move.</i>

627
00:32:41.791 --> 00:32:43.684
<i>Open war against me.</i>

628
00:32:43.708 --> 00:32:46.392
- [rock music plays over speakers]
- [lively chatter]

629
00:32:46.416 --> 00:32:48.166
[Jud] <i>I got pretty toasty.</i>

630
00:32:48.750 --> 00:32:51.559
The world's a wolf. You devil.

631
00:32:51.583 --> 00:32:53.291
You devil wolf.

632
00:32:53.791 --> 00:32:55.208
You're a devil wolf.

633
00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:57.208
Shit.

634
00:32:57.708 --> 00:33:01.226
- Oh! Nikolai, I'm sorry. I broke that...
- No, no, no. Don't worry about it.

635
00:33:01.250 --> 00:33:03.726
- ...with my hand.
- They're junk lamps. They're junk lamps.

636
00:33:03.750 --> 00:33:05.559
- Come on. Hey, are you good to drive?
- Yeah.

637
00:33:05.583 --> 00:33:06.476
Are you sure?

638
00:33:06.500 --> 00:33:08.583
[suspenseful music plays]

639
00:33:20.958 --> 00:33:22.601
- [grunts]
- [glass shatters]

640
00:33:22.625 --> 00:33:23.833
- [Sam] Hey!
- [gasps]

641
00:33:26.625 --> 00:33:27.809
Who's there?

642
00:33:27.833 --> 00:33:28.875
Oh, shit.

643
00:33:30.333 --> 00:33:32.458
[Jud] <i>And that brings us to Good Friday.</i>

644
00:33:34.625 --> 00:33:35.976
<i>Here we go.</i>

645
00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:38.208
<i>It was a 3:00 p.m. service.</i>

646
00:33:39.125 --> 00:33:40.375
<i>Just the regulars.</i>

647
00:33:41.208 --> 00:33:43.000
<i>There was a strange tension in the air.</i>

648
00:33:43.708 --> 00:33:46.000
<i>I can't recall the homily,
but it felt different.</i>

649
00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:49.875
<i>The anger felt less calculated.</i>

650
00:33:50.875 --> 00:33:51.767
<i>More unhinged.</i>

651
00:33:51.791 --> 00:33:54.833
- [imperceptible]
- [suspenseful music continues]

652
00:33:57.708 --> 00:34:01.351
[Jud] <i>As always, after the homily,
Wicks was spent,</i>

653
00:34:01.375 --> 00:34:04.666
<i>emotionally and physically,
and needed some time to recover.</i>

654
00:34:06.708 --> 00:34:09.017
<i>He would duck into
the small storage closet</i>

655
00:34:09.041 --> 00:34:12.000
<i>just to the side of the sanctuary
so he'd be out of sight.</i>

656
00:34:13.208 --> 00:34:16.267
<i>He'd fortify himself,
and I would continue the service</i>

657
00:34:16.291 --> 00:34:19.517
<i>until he felt strong enough
to rejoin and take over.</i>

658
00:34:19.541 --> 00:34:22.434
<i>♪ Behold the wood of the cross ♪</i>

659
00:34:22.458 --> 00:34:25.601
<i>♪ On which hung
The salvation of the world ♪</i>

660
00:34:25.625 --> 00:34:27.125
- [thudding]
- [object clangs]

661
00:34:34.458 --> 00:34:35.684
[Jud] Monsignor?

662
00:34:35.708 --> 00:34:38.208
[low suspenseful music plays]

663
00:34:50.166 --> 00:34:51.250
What's wrong?

664
00:34:53.083 --> 00:34:54.125
Jefferson?

665
00:34:57.000 --> 00:34:59.000
[suspenseful music rises]

666
00:35:16.583 --> 00:35:17.583
Jud.

667
00:35:20.625 --> 00:35:21.833
There's something in his back.

668
00:35:22.416 --> 00:35:23.583
[Nat] Wait. Don't touch it.

669
00:35:24.500 --> 00:35:25.916
Don't... don't touch anything.

670
00:35:36.875 --> 00:35:38.684
[music intensifies]

671
00:35:38.708 --> 00:35:39.958
[screaming]

672
00:35:44.458 --> 00:35:46.559
Satan! Satan struck him down!

673
00:35:46.583 --> 00:35:48.142
- The devil... Satan...
- [Vera] Martha.

674
00:35:48.166 --> 00:35:49.809
- ...struck him down!
- Oh, no.

675
00:35:49.833 --> 00:35:52.708
- [Martha screaming]
- [Vera] Martha! Please.

676
00:35:57.208 --> 00:35:59.208
[sinister dramatic music plays]

677
00:36:12.458 --> 00:36:13.458
[music stops]

678
00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:18.291
[fire crackling]

679
00:36:19.791 --> 00:36:21.416
[thunder rumbles in distance]

680
00:36:29.500 --> 00:36:32.059
[Jud] <i>The ambulance took
just five minutes to arrive.</i>

681
00:36:32.083 --> 00:36:34.351
- [sirens chirp]
- [sirens wailing]

682
00:36:34.375 --> 00:36:36.583
[Jud] <i>Wicks was pronounced dead
on the scene.</i>

683
00:36:37.291 --> 00:36:40.892
<i>When I joined the others outside,
the police were just arriving.</i>

684
00:36:40.916 --> 00:36:43.809
[Martha] The devil will not take
that man. [sniffles]

685
00:36:43.833 --> 00:36:46.750
He will rise again
in the glory of the Lord.

686
00:36:47.750 --> 00:36:50.559
[Jud] <i>This was an insane event
for a tiny town,</i>

687
00:36:50.583 --> 00:36:54.642
<i>and poor Geraldine, the local chief,
was thrown in headlong.</i>

688
00:36:54.666 --> 00:36:56.166
Christ.

689
00:36:57.375 --> 00:37:00.583
<i>Hours of questioning
all through the day Saturday</i>

690
00:37:01.250 --> 00:37:03.934
<i>- until finally...</i>
- [Geraldine] You're the only one on stage

691
00:37:03.958 --> 00:37:06.291
with the Monsignor
at the time of his killing.

692
00:37:07.041 --> 00:37:09.142
And you had prior possession
of the wolf-head figurine

693
00:37:09.166 --> 00:37:10.666
attached to the murder weapon.

694
00:37:12.500 --> 00:37:14.934
And you're the only one
at that church who hated his guts.

695
00:37:14.958 --> 00:37:16.851
I... I don't hate any guts.

696
00:37:16.875 --> 00:37:22.166
But it's literally impossible for anyone
to have done this, so I don't...

697
00:37:23.750 --> 00:37:26.833
[sighing] Oh, I don't know what this is.

698
00:37:31.250 --> 00:37:34.226
All right. Father,
why don't you go get some rest?

699
00:37:34.250 --> 00:37:37.208
But I should warn you before
you walk out of here, the town is talking.

700
00:37:37.833 --> 00:37:40.309
Cy Draven put this up
on his YouTube this morning.

701
00:37:40.333 --> 00:37:42.517
[Jud on recording]
<i>...poisoning this church.</i>

702
00:37:42.541 --> 00:37:44.750
<i>And I'll do whatever it takes to save it.</i>

703
00:37:45.291 --> 00:37:49.125
<i>To cut you out like a cancer.</i>

704
00:37:50.958 --> 00:37:53.333
- [exhales]
- [Geraldine] It's been reposted.

705
00:37:54.833 --> 00:37:56.017
Quite a bit.

706
00:37:56.041 --> 00:37:58.125
[intriguing music plays]

707
00:37:59.083 --> 00:38:00.958
[indistinct police radio chatter]

708
00:38:03.166 --> 00:38:04.666
[object crunches]

709
00:38:09.416 --> 00:38:11.416
[music turns suspenseful]

710
00:38:31.208 --> 00:38:35.625
[Jud] <i>I turned on my phone
for two minutes, and that was a mistake.</i>

711
00:38:36.166 --> 00:38:39.666
<i>A flood of messages to the killer priest.</i>

712
00:38:41.875 --> 00:38:45.541
<i>But I wasn't thinking about
getting arrested or defrocked.</i>

713
00:38:46.083 --> 00:38:49.916
<i>I was thinking Wicks had won.</i>

714
00:38:51.125 --> 00:38:54.017
<i>Because in the part of my soul
that cannot lie to Christ,</i>

715
00:38:54.041 --> 00:38:56.934
<i>or myself, or you...</i>

716
00:38:56.958 --> 00:38:58.309
[door creaking]

717
00:38:58.333 --> 00:38:59.625
[door thuds]

718
00:39:00.333 --> 00:39:03.392
<i>...I was happy the old man was dead.</i>

719
00:39:03.416 --> 00:39:05.333
[solemn music plays]

720
00:39:07.625 --> 00:39:08.708
[exhales]

721
00:39:11.791 --> 00:39:12.875
Jesus.

722
00:39:13.708 --> 00:39:16.291
Help me. [sniffles]

723
00:39:17.333 --> 00:39:19.750
Show me the way through this, please.

724
00:39:23.208 --> 00:39:24.059
[music fades]

725
00:39:24.083 --> 00:39:25.666
[man] Hello?

726
00:39:29.416 --> 00:39:31.041
Oh, I'm sorry. Um...

727
00:39:32.500 --> 00:39:33.916
Are you open?

728
00:39:35.041 --> 00:39:36.125
Always.

729
00:39:36.708 --> 00:39:37.916
You all right?

730
00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:41.500
Yeah. Uh-huh. Sorry. [sniffles]

731
00:39:42.875 --> 00:39:47.767
- Uh, there's no Easter Mass. I'm sorry.
- Oh, I'm...

732
00:39:47.791 --> 00:39:51.517
- You're welcome. Come in. Come in.
- Thank you. Thank you.

733
00:39:51.541 --> 00:39:56.041
I don't want to take you away
from your priestly duties now, do I?

734
00:39:56.833 --> 00:39:57.833
[Benoit clicks tongue]

735
00:39:58.416 --> 00:40:01.142
Well... [chuckles] Isn't this something?

736
00:40:01.166 --> 00:40:02.059
[Jud] Right?

737
00:40:02.083 --> 00:40:06.375
It's... hard to be in here
and not feel His presence.

738
00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:08.083
Whose?

739
00:40:09.041 --> 00:40:12.059
Oh. God... Oh. Yeah. [laughs]

740
00:40:12.083 --> 00:40:13.726
- Yeah.
- You're not a Catholic.

741
00:40:13.750 --> 00:40:16.000
No, very much not, no. [chuckles]

742
00:40:16.875 --> 00:40:18.291
Proud heretic.

743
00:40:19.250 --> 00:40:21.434
I kneel at the altar of the rational.

744
00:40:21.458 --> 00:40:24.500
Uh-huh. You weren't raised in the faith?

745
00:40:25.250 --> 00:40:27.934
My mother is... was, uh,

746
00:40:27.958 --> 00:40:30.875
very, very religious, you know.

747
00:40:32.583 --> 00:40:34.416
- Were you close with her?
- No.

748
00:40:35.250 --> 00:40:39.101
When I was a boy, we, um... But it's...

749
00:40:39.125 --> 00:40:41.184
- Complicated. Family.
- [chuckles] It's complicated.

750
00:40:41.208 --> 00:40:42.708
Yeah. That's right.

751
00:40:47.791 --> 00:40:49.500
How does all this make you feel?

752
00:40:52.791 --> 00:40:54.625
How does it make me feel?

753
00:40:59.208 --> 00:41:00.017
Truthfully?

754
00:41:00.041 --> 00:41:01.208
Sure.

755
00:41:03.541 --> 00:41:05.375
Well, the architecture, that interests me.

756
00:41:06.375 --> 00:41:09.559
I feel the grandeur, the... the mystery,

757
00:41:09.583 --> 00:41:13.375
the intended emotional effect.
It's... [exhales]

758
00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:20.958
And it's like someone has shone
a story at me that I do not believe.

759
00:41:21.833 --> 00:41:25.476
It's built upon the empty promise
of a child's fairy tale

760
00:41:25.500 --> 00:41:30.226
filled with malevolence
and misogyny and homophobia

761
00:41:30.250 --> 00:41:34.476
and its justified
untold acts of violence and cruelty

762
00:41:34.500 --> 00:41:36.559
while all the while, and still,

763
00:41:36.583 --> 00:41:39.476
hiding its own shameful acts.

764
00:41:39.500 --> 00:41:41.684
So like an ornery mule kicking back,

765
00:41:41.708 --> 00:41:44.976
I want to pick it apart
and pop its perfidious bubble of belief

766
00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:50.291
and get to a truth
I can swallow without choking.

767
00:41:57.708 --> 00:42:02.833
[spluttering] The rafter details
are very fine, though. It's...

768
00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:07.309
Listen... you want to kick me out,
you go right ahead.

769
00:42:07.333 --> 00:42:08.500
No, no.

770
00:42:09.958 --> 00:42:11.601
You're being honest, it's good.

771
00:42:11.625 --> 00:42:15.142
- Telling the truth can be a belly rub.
- [chuckles softly]

772
00:42:15.166 --> 00:42:18.976
Now, I suspect you can't always
be honest with your parishioners.

773
00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:22.250
You can always be honest
by not saying the unhonest part.

774
00:42:23.000 --> 00:42:24.875
Yeah. [laughs]

775
00:42:26.125 --> 00:42:28.125
You're right. It's storytelling.

776
00:42:29.416 --> 00:42:32.958
And this church, it's... it's not medieval.
We're in New York.

777
00:42:33.708 --> 00:42:35.809
It's neo-Gothic 19th century.

778
00:42:35.833 --> 00:42:38.434
It has more in common
with Disneyland than Notre-Dame...

779
00:42:38.458 --> 00:42:40.750
- [chuckles]
- ...and the rites and rituals

780
00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:43.625
and costumes, all of it.

781
00:42:44.458 --> 00:42:45.875
It's storytelling.

782
00:42:47.333 --> 00:42:48.416
You're right.

783
00:42:51.291 --> 00:42:52.916
I guess the question is

784
00:42:54.625 --> 00:42:56.750
do these stories convince us of a lie?

785
00:42:58.625 --> 00:43:02.708
Or do they resonate with something
deep inside us that's profoundly true?

786
00:43:05.458 --> 00:43:08.125
[clicks tongue] That we can't express
any other way...

787
00:43:10.416 --> 00:43:11.833
except storytelling.

788
00:43:15.125 --> 00:43:17.333
Touché. Padre.

789
00:43:19.208 --> 00:43:22.833
[laughs, sobs]

790
00:43:25.833 --> 00:43:27.250
- Son.
- I'm sorry.

791
00:43:28.416 --> 00:43:29.625
I just...

792
00:43:30.875 --> 00:43:33.291
I just felt like a priest again,

793
00:43:33.916 --> 00:43:36.041
and now I'm gonna lose that and...

794
00:43:37.000 --> 00:43:39.517
and lose my purpose
and I'm frightened, I...

795
00:43:39.541 --> 00:43:42.101
- I don't know how I'll live.
- [door opens]

796
00:43:42.125 --> 00:43:43.291
[Geraldine] Blanc!

797
00:43:44.041 --> 00:43:46.642
Hey! You found him! Is he...

798
00:43:46.666 --> 00:43:49.291
[indistinct police radio chatter]

799
00:43:52.708 --> 00:43:54.458
- [door closes]
- [chuckles]

800
00:43:55.208 --> 00:43:56.184
Who are you?

801
00:43:56.208 --> 00:43:57.892
I probably should have led with this.

802
00:43:57.916 --> 00:44:00.059
My name is Benoit Blanc. I'm a detective.

803
00:44:00.083 --> 00:44:03.583
I've taken an interest
in the murder of Jefferson Wicks.

804
00:44:04.958 --> 00:44:06.767
You're a detective,
so you're with the police?

805
00:44:06.791 --> 00:44:09.059
No, no. I work in a private capacity.

806
00:44:09.083 --> 00:44:12.250
Everyone thinks I did it. I didn't do it.

807
00:44:12.833 --> 00:44:14.851
But in my heart maybe I did,

808
00:44:14.875 --> 00:44:17.434
and the way it happened
was some kind of miracle.

809
00:44:17.458 --> 00:44:18.541
And...

810
00:44:19.041 --> 00:44:21.541
I don't know. I'm lost. I don't know.

811
00:44:22.708 --> 00:44:24.708
Would you allow me to help you?

812
00:44:27.041 --> 00:44:28.101
What?

813
00:44:28.125 --> 00:44:30.934
Your lips are cracked with dehydration.

814
00:44:30.958 --> 00:44:32.458
You haven't slept all night.

815
00:44:33.041 --> 00:44:34.392
You spent it out of doors,

816
00:44:34.416 --> 00:44:37.333
by the state of your pant legs,
on your knees in prayer.

817
00:44:38.083 --> 00:44:41.500
What I see is not a guilty man in torment,

818
00:44:42.250 --> 00:44:45.458
but an innocent man tormented by guilt.

819
00:44:47.083 --> 00:44:48.291
Let me help you.

820
00:44:48.833 --> 00:44:49.684
How?

821
00:44:49.708 --> 00:44:51.708
This was dressed as a miracle.

822
00:44:52.208 --> 00:44:55.625
It's just a murder and I solve murders.

823
00:44:56.125 --> 00:44:57.392
[inhales]

824
00:44:57.416 --> 00:44:59.666
Wait... [hesitates] Were you

825
00:45:00.500 --> 00:45:03.642
the Kentucky Derby thing with the murder
and the guy that they caught

826
00:45:03.666 --> 00:45:05.226
- with the photo-finish camera?
- Yeah.

827
00:45:05.250 --> 00:45:06.416
So you're...

828
00:45:07.250 --> 00:45:08.434
You were on <i>The View.</i>

829
00:45:08.458 --> 00:45:10.142
- Yeah.
- What are you doing here?

830
00:45:10.166 --> 00:45:13.976
I need to come up to speed rapidly
on the events of that Good Friday service

831
00:45:14.000 --> 00:45:17.041
and the goings-on here
at Our Lady of Perpetual Fortitude.

832
00:45:17.708 --> 00:45:20.559
Geraldine has graciously
allowed me access.

833
00:45:20.583 --> 00:45:24.750
If you can spare the day
and accompany me on my investigation,

834
00:45:25.375 --> 00:45:28.517
view the body, trace the murder weapon,
inspect the crime scene,

835
00:45:28.541 --> 00:45:32.125
you are in a unique position to assist me.

836
00:45:33.791 --> 00:45:35.000
The body?

837
00:45:35.583 --> 00:45:37.583
[unsettling string music plays]

838
00:45:38.250 --> 00:45:39.375
[music fades]

839
00:45:40.916 --> 00:45:43.625
Yeah, I've changed my mind.
I don't think I should be here.

840
00:45:44.708 --> 00:45:47.309
Blanc, I'm trusting your process,
but I agree with him.

841
00:45:47.333 --> 00:45:51.476
No, no, no. I want you to get a clear
clinical picture of what happened here.

842
00:45:51.500 --> 00:45:55.642
To see Wicks as he is, a corpse.
Just an empty vessel.

843
00:45:55.666 --> 00:45:59.184
Not the mythologized monster in your mind,
but merely flesh and blood.

844
00:45:59.208 --> 00:46:01.684
Dead from a knife wound we can analyze.

845
00:46:01.708 --> 00:46:04.541
Just... flesh and blood.

846
00:46:05.208 --> 00:46:06.476
Flesh and blood, yeah.

847
00:46:06.500 --> 00:46:09.250
Yes, that's our wiggly wiggly. [chuckles]

848
00:46:09.833 --> 00:46:12.041
- Yeah. Look at that.
- Please stop doing that.

849
00:46:13.125 --> 00:46:15.767
- Tammy. Would you mind flipping the meat?
- [Tammy] Mm.

850
00:46:15.791 --> 00:46:17.392
- Pancake him. Yeah.
- [Benoit] Mm-hmm.

851
00:46:17.416 --> 00:46:18.541
[Geraldine clears throat]

852
00:46:21.958 --> 00:46:23.559
[Tammy, muffled] One, two, three.

853
00:46:23.583 --> 00:46:24.625
[body thuds]

854
00:46:26.000 --> 00:46:28.458
[panting]

855
00:46:29.500 --> 00:46:32.601
- Hey! Hey.
- No, no, no. I don't belong here.

856
00:46:32.625 --> 00:46:35.059
I can't be in here,
I don't know why you brought me.

857
00:46:35.083 --> 00:46:36.208
- I can't--
- Hey!

858
00:46:37.416 --> 00:46:38.809
If you want absolution,

859
00:46:38.833 --> 00:46:42.351
you want ever to be a priest again,
then you need to go through this with me.

860
00:46:42.375 --> 00:46:46.101
The real killer is out there.
We've got to find him, nail him, I...

861
00:46:46.125 --> 00:46:47.017
I'm sorry.

862
00:46:47.041 --> 00:46:50.017
- I mean, catch him and get your life back.
- [door opens, closes]

863
00:46:50.041 --> 00:46:51.333
Father.

864
00:46:52.083 --> 00:46:54.392
I need to know
you understand the situation.

865
00:46:54.416 --> 00:46:57.809
We are not all buddies
running around trying to solve a case.

866
00:46:57.833 --> 00:46:59.517
You are still a suspect.

867
00:46:59.541 --> 00:47:03.684
The point is, you do not need to be here
without a lawyer, do you understand that?

868
00:47:03.708 --> 00:47:05.476
[intriguing music plays]

869
00:47:05.500 --> 00:47:08.333
[breathing heavily] I didn't do this.

870
00:47:09.583 --> 00:47:11.250
If I can help find out who did,

871
00:47:11.916 --> 00:47:14.434
then I'm in, yeah, let's do it, yeah.

872
00:47:14.458 --> 00:47:15.750
Okay.

873
00:47:16.625 --> 00:47:17.809
The body.

874
00:47:17.833 --> 00:47:19.476
Next the murder weapon.

875
00:47:19.500 --> 00:47:20.666
Then the crime scene.

876
00:47:21.958 --> 00:47:23.375
Stick with me.

877
00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:26.166
[intriguing string music plays]

878
00:47:29.000 --> 00:47:30.017
[music stops]

879
00:47:30.041 --> 00:47:32.726
I'll tell you something,
I don't even like the devil.

880
00:47:32.750 --> 00:47:36.892
You know, Il Diavolo, it sounds classy,
Italian, that's fine, you know,

881
00:47:36.916 --> 00:47:39.101
then my wife, she buys a devil sign,

882
00:47:39.125 --> 00:47:41.101
then she buys the devil lamps

883
00:47:41.125 --> 00:47:44.767
and then people start, "Oh, hey, get him
a devil thing for the bar, he loves it."

884
00:47:44.791 --> 00:47:48.476
And then, you know, uh,
devil, devil, devil, bang, I don't know.

885
00:47:48.500 --> 00:47:50.934
- [Benoit] But that... But that's it, right?
- [Nikolai] Yeah.

886
00:47:50.958 --> 00:47:52.142
[Benoit] Oh, yeah.

887
00:47:52.166 --> 00:47:55.101
- [rock music plays faintly over speakers]
- [Nikolai] But, uh...

888
00:47:55.125 --> 00:47:58.684
But you know, it wasn't red though,
it's red now, it's paint.

889
00:47:58.708 --> 00:48:01.166
- Yes, yes, freshly painted.
- I hope. Hmm.

890
00:48:01.750 --> 00:48:05.351
They filled it with some kind of plaster
and stuck the blade in that way.

891
00:48:05.375 --> 00:48:07.809
[Jud on recording] <i>I'll do
whatever it takes to save it.</i>

892
00:48:07.833 --> 00:48:11.101
<i>To cut you out like a cancer.</i>

893
00:48:11.125 --> 00:48:14.434
[Nikolai] Hey, hey, hey. Come on, ixnay.
That's not cool. Come on.

894
00:48:14.458 --> 00:48:16.809
The red devil head thing,
that ended up where?

895
00:48:16.833 --> 00:48:21.476
In the church, I threw it at the church
and it broke a window, I don't know why.

896
00:48:21.500 --> 00:48:22.976
- [church bell tolling]
- [gasps]

897
00:48:23.000 --> 00:48:25.267
[Jud] <i>And then
after the Chrism Mass on Monday,</i>

898
00:48:25.291 --> 00:48:27.684
<i>Martha said she found
a small broken window.</i>

899
00:48:27.708 --> 00:48:28.767
Kids.

900
00:48:28.791 --> 00:48:30.267
But nothing else.

901
00:48:30.291 --> 00:48:33.166
[over speakers] <i>♪ I know what's wrong
I don't confuse it ♪</i>

902
00:48:34.541 --> 00:48:36.726
<i>♪ All I'm really trying to say is ♪</i>

903
00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:39.250
<i>♪ Why should the devil have
All the good music? ♪</i>

904
00:48:39.916 --> 00:48:42.333
<i>♪ I feel good every day ♪</i>

905
00:48:44.166 --> 00:48:47.583
<i>♪ Because Jesus is the rock
And he rolled my blues away ♪</i>

906
00:48:48.666 --> 00:48:49.934
[Benoit] Do you see that?

907
00:48:49.958 --> 00:48:52.708
<i>♪ They say to cut my hair
They're driving me insane... ♪</i>

908
00:48:53.583 --> 00:48:54.583
[Benoit] Hmm.

909
00:48:56.083 --> 00:48:57.083
Yeah.

910
00:48:58.625 --> 00:48:59.708
Yeah.

911
00:49:03.375 --> 00:49:05.375
Oh. That is, uh...

912
00:49:07.708 --> 00:49:08.916
Dr. Nat.

913
00:49:10.291 --> 00:49:11.500
Oh, f...

914
00:49:13.791 --> 00:49:15.875
[clears throat] Hey.

915
00:49:17.791 --> 00:49:19.208
Just having some lunch.

916
00:49:20.958 --> 00:49:21.958
[clears throat]

917
00:49:25.750 --> 00:49:29.351
Nat, I... I can come over later
if... if you want to talk.

918
00:49:29.375 --> 00:49:31.559
I don't think... Yeah, I... Yeah.

919
00:49:31.583 --> 00:49:33.559
I don't think I'd prefer that.

920
00:49:33.583 --> 00:49:35.083
I'd prefer not that.

921
00:49:35.833 --> 00:49:39.083
Ah, there it is.
Here's what you did it with. Huh?

922
00:49:39.625 --> 00:49:41.767
- Come on, Nat.
- "Cut him out like a cancer."

923
00:49:41.791 --> 00:49:43.642
You son of a bitch.

924
00:49:43.666 --> 00:49:45.375
- [man] You tell him, Nat.
- Yeah.

925
00:49:46.583 --> 00:49:49.708
- Son of a bitch! Killer priest!
- [door slams]

926
00:49:50.958 --> 00:49:53.583
[intriguing suspenseful music plays]

927
00:50:07.041 --> 00:50:08.521
- [thudding]
- [TV plays indistinctly]

928
00:50:22.166 --> 00:50:23.059
First off,

929
00:50:23.083 --> 00:50:26.101
I owe Detective Elliott a fruit basket
for giving me your number.

930
00:50:26.125 --> 00:50:28.017
I'm just glad to be of service.

931
00:50:28.041 --> 00:50:29.934
Geraldine, you had the foresight

932
00:50:29.958 --> 00:50:32.601
to see that this goes
way beyond normal police work,

933
00:50:32.625 --> 00:50:36.934
this is something
even I have not experienced.

934
00:50:36.958 --> 00:50:41.125
A textbook example
of a perfectly impossible crime.

935
00:50:41.958 --> 00:50:43.559
The stuff of detective fiction.

936
00:50:43.583 --> 00:50:47.000
This should not exist in our real world.

937
00:50:48.041 --> 00:50:49.458
And yet here it is.

938
00:50:50.000 --> 00:50:51.416
The holy grail.

939
00:50:52.500 --> 00:50:53.726
[Geraldine] I love the passion.

940
00:50:53.750 --> 00:50:56.309
I just need to feel confident
you know this case is solvable.

941
00:50:56.333 --> 00:50:58.708
[Benoit] I'm incapable
of not solving a crime.

942
00:50:59.458 --> 00:51:01.083
That moment of checkmate

943
00:51:01.958 --> 00:51:05.375
when I take the stage
and unravel my opponent's web...

944
00:51:07.125 --> 00:51:08.416
Oh, you'll see. It's fun!

945
00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:11.166
- Great, how do we get there?
- [Benoit] The source,

946
00:51:12.000 --> 00:51:14.583
John Dickson Carr's <i>The Hollow Man.</i>

947
00:51:15.375 --> 00:51:17.601
{\an8}A Golden Age detective novel

948
00:51:17.625 --> 00:51:19.809
and a veritable primer
on the locked-door mystery,

949
00:51:19.833 --> 00:51:21.208
the impossible crime.

950
00:51:22.958 --> 00:51:24.017
Hold up.

951
00:51:24.041 --> 00:51:26.125
[intriguing music plays]

952
00:51:32.375 --> 00:51:35.875
Father Jud, once again,
earning your keep. [chuckles]

953
00:51:37.541 --> 00:51:40.142
{\an8}<i>Whose Body?
The Murders in the Rue Morgue.</i>

954
00:51:40.166 --> 00:51:42.642
{\an8}<i>Roger Ackroyd.
The Murder at the Vicarage.</i>

955
00:51:42.666 --> 00:51:47.958
My God, this is practically
a syllabus for how to commit this crime.

956
00:51:48.458 --> 00:51:50.375
And the whole flock is in this group?

957
00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:55.625
Who chose these books?

958
00:51:56.291 --> 00:51:57.291
Oprah.

959
00:51:58.333 --> 00:51:59.333
Oprah.

960
00:51:59.833 --> 00:52:01.892
Martha pulls a theme list
from Oprah's site.

961
00:52:01.916 --> 00:52:03.833
Well, this confirms my theory.

962
00:52:04.416 --> 00:52:06.309
The killer certainly imitated

963
00:52:06.333 --> 00:52:09.166
the traditional methods
of a locked-door mystery story.

964
00:52:09.708 --> 00:52:11.892
Which makes things simple.

965
00:52:11.916 --> 00:52:13.208
Book club time.

966
00:52:13.750 --> 00:52:15.166
Come on, kids.

967
00:52:20.666 --> 00:52:21.726
[music fades]

968
00:52:21.750 --> 00:52:24.142
In <i>The Hollow Man,</i>
the detective Gideon Fell

969
00:52:24.166 --> 00:52:29.142
gives a rundown of all possible methods of
a locked-door killing. [chuckles smugly]

970
00:52:29.166 --> 00:52:31.351
So let's line them up and knock them down.

971
00:52:31.375 --> 00:52:33.559
Possibility number one,

972
00:52:33.583 --> 00:52:37.083
Wicks was stabbed with the knife

973
00:52:37.708 --> 00:52:40.041
before entering the closet.

974
00:52:40.625 --> 00:52:44.041
Father Jud, would you take your place
right where you were?

975
00:52:47.208 --> 00:52:49.333
Wicks completes his sermon.

976
00:52:51.083 --> 00:52:52.726
Any device behind him

977
00:52:52.750 --> 00:52:57.833
capable of propelling
a heavy unbalanced dagger into his back...

978
00:53:00.791 --> 00:53:04.875
would have been hidden from the camera
and the witnesses in the nave,

979
00:53:05.500 --> 00:53:09.000
but certainly would have been
witnessed by Father Jud.

980
00:53:10.625 --> 00:53:13.642
No, I didn't see
a knife-shooting robot behind him.

981
00:53:13.666 --> 00:53:16.267
[Benoit] No. Possibility one, nixed.

982
00:53:16.291 --> 00:53:18.208
Possibility two...

983
00:53:20.083 --> 00:53:21.500
He was killed

984
00:53:22.000 --> 00:53:23.809
while inside the closet

985
00:53:23.833 --> 00:53:27.541
by someone or something that was

986
00:53:28.291 --> 00:53:30.125
outside the closet.

987
00:53:30.833 --> 00:53:34.392
Like, something shot the knife
into the closet from out here?

988
00:53:34.416 --> 00:53:35.892
No, that's nuts.

989
00:53:35.916 --> 00:53:37.976
Nuts and impossible on several fronts.

990
00:53:38.000 --> 00:53:41.059
Possibility two, nixed. Progress!

991
00:53:41.083 --> 00:53:43.226
[sighs] How many more possibilities
are there?

992
00:53:43.250 --> 00:53:45.333
Not many. Three...

993
00:53:46.166 --> 00:53:49.017
He was killed while inside the closet

994
00:53:49.041 --> 00:53:51.750
by a device that was also

995
00:53:53.041 --> 00:53:54.517
inside the closet.

996
00:53:54.541 --> 00:53:56.809
So something was placed beforehand and,

997
00:53:56.833 --> 00:53:58.517
what, triggered with a remote?

998
00:53:58.541 --> 00:54:00.601
Well, these walls are thick,

999
00:54:00.625 --> 00:54:04.125
but a very strong RF signal
could blast through 'em.

1000
00:54:04.875 --> 00:54:07.083
- So where would one hide...
- [lightbulb clinks]

1001
00:54:07.625 --> 00:54:12.041
...a fully functional
remote-controlled knife-propelling device

1002
00:54:12.583 --> 00:54:14.333
in an empty box, mm?

1003
00:54:15.125 --> 00:54:16.851
Wicks fell on his stomach.

1004
00:54:16.875 --> 00:54:19.642
- [intriguing music plays]
- I remember that clangy thud.

1005
00:54:19.666 --> 00:54:20.833
- [thudding]
- [object clangs]

1006
00:54:21.583 --> 00:54:24.059
[Jud] <i>And his face was towards the door,</i>

1007
00:54:24.083 --> 00:54:26.125
so he was standing at the back facing out,

1008
00:54:27.166 --> 00:54:30.583
so the knife must have
somehow come through the back wall.

1009
00:54:31.083 --> 00:54:33.500
Clangy thud. Very good.

1010
00:54:34.041 --> 00:54:35.250
However,

1011
00:54:36.250 --> 00:54:38.125
Rock of Gibraltar.

1012
00:54:38.958 --> 00:54:40.041
What about...

1013
00:54:40.541 --> 00:54:43.101
What about a false wall
that was removed later?

1014
00:54:43.125 --> 00:54:45.142
Go to town, Father Brown.

1015
00:54:45.166 --> 00:54:46.684
[Geraldine] What? No, no.

1016
00:54:46.708 --> 00:54:48.851
My boys would have noticed a fake wall.

1017
00:54:48.875 --> 00:54:51.541
[switch clicking]

1018
00:54:52.750 --> 00:54:56.583
Yeah. And removing it later
would have been no small task.

1019
00:54:58.416 --> 00:55:01.166
Nothing else was found
on the floor of this space, yes?

1020
00:55:01.833 --> 00:55:04.458
Nope. No, just the red thread.

1021
00:55:06.500 --> 00:55:07.892
Wait, what? The...

1022
00:55:07.916 --> 00:55:09.684
The red thread?

1023
00:55:09.708 --> 00:55:11.559
[music rises, fades]

1024
00:55:11.583 --> 00:55:15.267
Two strings of thick red thread
about three inches long, found there

1025
00:55:15.291 --> 00:55:17.375
over by the body next to the hip.

1026
00:55:19.333 --> 00:55:20.916
So, what's possibility four?

1027
00:55:22.375 --> 00:55:25.333
[Martha sobbing]

1028
00:55:26.125 --> 00:55:27.208
Martha?

1029
00:55:28.166 --> 00:55:30.351
- [Martha] Throw them out.
- [Jud] Martha?

1030
00:55:30.375 --> 00:55:31.726
[Martha] Throw them out!

1031
00:55:31.750 --> 00:55:35.059
[sobbing] To walk this holy place

1032
00:55:35.083 --> 00:55:38.476
like some crime scene. Like some

1033
00:55:38.500 --> 00:55:42.208
tawdry police show talking of robots!

1034
00:55:42.791 --> 00:55:46.267
It's not right, Father,
it isn't, it's not right!

1035
00:55:46.291 --> 00:55:47.684
[door opens]

1036
00:55:47.708 --> 00:55:48.809
[door closes]

1037
00:55:48.833 --> 00:55:51.541
Martha, you should go home, get some rest.

1038
00:55:52.083 --> 00:55:53.208
Can I do anything for you?

1039
00:55:54.041 --> 00:55:55.083
Leave.

1040
00:55:55.791 --> 00:55:57.625
No one wants you here anymore.

1041
00:55:58.125 --> 00:56:02.976
You've always hated the Monsignor
and you have nothing but contempt for us.

1042
00:56:03.000 --> 00:56:05.476
- That's not true.
- Murder in your heart...

1043
00:56:05.500 --> 00:56:07.184
- No.
- ...blood on your hands

1044
00:56:07.208 --> 00:56:09.267
just like the harlot whore.

1045
00:56:09.291 --> 00:56:12.583
Your original sin has stained this place.

1046
00:56:13.333 --> 00:56:15.083
False priest!

1047
00:56:17.958 --> 00:56:19.541
- Sorry.
- [gasps softly]

1048
00:56:20.375 --> 00:56:22.517
But, yeah,
if finding the facts with that detective

1049
00:56:22.541 --> 00:56:24.791
puts me against you
and this flock then so be it.

1050
00:56:27.583 --> 00:56:28.666
Sorry.

1051
00:56:31.000 --> 00:56:32.958
[music turns melancholic]

1052
00:56:36.541 --> 00:56:38.226
[grunting]

1053
00:56:38.250 --> 00:56:39.434
[music fades]

1054
00:56:39.458 --> 00:56:41.291
Can I use your bag? [grunting]

1055
00:56:42.791 --> 00:56:44.392
Sure. Go ahead.

1056
00:56:44.416 --> 00:56:47.250
- [continues grunting]
- [TV plays indistinctly]

1057
00:56:47.958 --> 00:56:49.375
Are you okay?

1058
00:56:50.000 --> 00:56:53.351
[Jud] <i>A fog was clearing.
This was a puzzle that was solvable.</i>

1059
00:56:53.375 --> 00:56:56.059
<i>The body, the weapon,
the crime scene, robot-knife guns,</i>

1060
00:56:56.083 --> 00:56:59.184
<i>and angles of view
and stone walls and remote controls.</i>

1061
00:56:59.208 --> 00:57:01.559
- [panting]
- [organ music plays on TV]

1062
00:57:01.583 --> 00:57:02.934
<i>And remote controls.</i>

1063
00:57:02.958 --> 00:57:06.476
[commentary on TV] <i>But it's a new day...
The Cubs...</i>

1064
00:57:06.500 --> 00:57:09.309
<i>who would have been
the hero in that game...</i>

1065
00:57:09.333 --> 00:57:11.684
<i>But we saw how that went...</i>

1066
00:57:11.708 --> 00:57:14.208
You didn't listen to the game
during Friday's service.

1067
00:57:14.875 --> 00:57:16.392
On your radio.

1068
00:57:16.416 --> 00:57:20.083
- I would not. Martha doesn't approve.
- [commentary continues]

1069
00:57:21.875 --> 00:57:23.291
So you taped it.

1070
00:57:23.958 --> 00:57:25.041
Yep.

1071
00:57:26.708 --> 00:57:30.767
Okay. I've overlaid a time stamp
taking into account the broadcast delay.

1072
00:57:30.791 --> 00:57:35.101
Dr. Sharp's call to the hospital
happened at 3:47 p.m.

1073
00:57:35.125 --> 00:57:37.601
and 90 seconds before that...

1074
00:57:37.625 --> 00:57:39.000
[keyboard clacking]

1075
00:57:43.625 --> 00:57:46.142
So, several things
could have caused that glitch,

1076
00:57:46.166 --> 00:57:47.601
but to answer your question, yes,

1077
00:57:47.625 --> 00:57:51.184
it is consistent
with a strong burst of RF interference

1078
00:57:51.208 --> 00:57:53.809
that you might get
from a souped-up remote control.

1079
00:57:53.833 --> 00:57:55.476
- [chuckles in relief]
- Damn.

1080
00:57:55.500 --> 00:57:56.476
This is it, right?

1081
00:57:56.500 --> 00:57:58.434
- This triggered the knife robot.
- [sucks teeth]

1082
00:57:58.458 --> 00:58:01.309
This has got to be it.
You can... You can solve it now, right?

1083
00:58:01.333 --> 00:58:03.809
Did you, um...
Did you sync this up with Cy's footage?

1084
00:58:03.833 --> 00:58:07.642
I did, yeah. The iPhone video
has a time stamp. It's very precise.

1085
00:58:07.666 --> 00:58:09.726
- Okay. Can you show us please?
- Yeah.

1086
00:58:09.750 --> 00:58:10.851
Show us what?

1087
00:58:10.875 --> 00:58:13.809
{\an8}[Jud] <i>♪ On which hung
The salvation of the world ♪</i>

1088
00:58:13.833 --> 00:58:15.791
{\an8}- [thudding]
- [object clangs]

1089
00:58:16.875 --> 00:58:17.958
{\an8}[Jud] <i>Monsignor?</i>

1090
00:58:23.916 --> 00:58:24.916
{\an8}[keyboard clacks]

1091
00:58:29.333 --> 00:58:30.767
- Oh.
- [Benoit groans]

1092
00:58:30.791 --> 00:58:32.976
So when the RF burst happened,

1093
00:58:33.000 --> 00:58:35.916
{\an8}he was already on the ground
with a knife in his back

1094
00:58:36.541 --> 00:58:38.875
{\an8}and Father Jud was staring right at him.

1095
00:58:39.375 --> 00:58:41.142
How does that work?

1096
00:58:41.166 --> 00:58:43.934
Well, for the knife robot, it doesn't.

1097
00:58:43.958 --> 00:58:46.601
So... this was nothing?

1098
00:58:46.625 --> 00:58:47.601
[Benoit] No, no.

1099
00:58:47.625 --> 00:58:51.017
This was very much something.
[inhales deeply]

1100
00:58:51.041 --> 00:58:53.934
We have all the pieces
laid out before us now.

1101
00:58:53.958 --> 00:58:55.142
- We do?
- Yeah.

1102
00:58:55.166 --> 00:58:58.434
Consider the origin
of the devil's head, the red thread,

1103
00:58:58.458 --> 00:59:01.958
the clangy clunk,
the timing of the RF remote.

1104
00:59:02.958 --> 00:59:04.375
It all lines up.

1105
00:59:05.500 --> 00:59:07.708
[chuckles uncertainly]
So give us the answer.

1106
00:59:08.916 --> 00:59:10.208
I can't.

1107
00:59:11.666 --> 00:59:15.184
You said if you had all the pieces,
you would have an answer.

1108
00:59:15.208 --> 00:59:16.517
[Benoit] I know. And yet,

1109
00:59:16.541 --> 00:59:18.684
with all the pieces on the table,

1110
00:59:18.708 --> 00:59:21.958
this crime still truly appears

1111
00:59:22.750 --> 00:59:23.958
impossible.

1112
00:59:24.791 --> 00:59:26.476
You told me you could solve this.

1113
00:59:26.500 --> 00:59:28.434
[solemn music plays]

1114
00:59:28.458 --> 00:59:30.041
That's what you do.

1115
00:59:31.083 --> 00:59:33.642
[sighs] I put my faith in you.

1116
00:59:33.666 --> 00:59:37.226
Oh, God. Oh, God. [sighs]

1117
00:59:37.250 --> 00:59:41.351
[Benoit] You haven't slept in 36 hours.
I think it's bedtime. [clears throat]

1118
00:59:41.375 --> 00:59:42.541
Yeah.

1119
00:59:43.166 --> 00:59:45.851
It's been a hell of a day.
Good night, Father Jud.

1120
00:59:45.875 --> 00:59:47.500
Come on. I'll drive you back.

1121
00:59:48.375 --> 00:59:50.375
[intriguing music plays]

1122
00:59:56.833 --> 00:59:59.351
- [music fades]
- You know, you're right.

1123
00:59:59.375 --> 01:00:02.083
This can't be impossible.

1124
01:00:03.333 --> 01:00:05.166
There must be a piece missing.

1125
01:00:06.458 --> 01:00:08.458
And I think I know where to find it.

1126
01:00:09.041 --> 01:00:11.767
See, I think there's something in there,
in your head,

1127
01:00:11.791 --> 01:00:14.291
that I need to solve this case.

1128
01:00:14.958 --> 01:00:17.791
And if I can't shake it loose, I'm sorry,

1129
01:00:18.333 --> 01:00:20.750
but I'm gonna have to
go in there and get it.

1130
01:00:23.250 --> 01:00:25.166
Okay, you're freaking me out now.

1131
01:00:27.250 --> 01:00:29.476
- I don't... I don't understand.
- [door closes]

1132
01:00:29.500 --> 01:00:32.517
{\an8}I don't get how this will help.
You want me to write the story?

1133
01:00:32.541 --> 01:00:34.809
{\an8}Yes, the story.

1134
01:00:34.833 --> 01:00:37.601
{\an8}The story of the Reverend Wicks' murder.

1135
01:00:37.625 --> 01:00:38.767
{\an8}Monsignor Wicks.

1136
01:00:38.791 --> 01:00:40.684
{\an8}Monsignor Jalapeño. I don't care.

1137
01:00:40.708 --> 01:00:44.184
{\an8}I need to see his murder and the events
leading up to it through your eyes.

1138
01:00:44.208 --> 01:00:45.142
{\an8}Starting where?

1139
01:00:45.166 --> 01:00:46.309
{\an8}Oh, wherever you like.

1140
01:00:46.333 --> 01:00:49.601
Just keep it interesting,
keep it moving and spare no detail.

1141
01:00:49.625 --> 01:00:50.976
Blanc, I'm not a writer.

1142
01:00:51.000 --> 01:00:53.083
Take your time. Oh.

1143
01:00:53.625 --> 01:00:56.351
{\an8}- [chuckles] I'll be quite comfortable.
- [cork squeaks]

1144
01:00:56.375 --> 01:00:57.642
{\an8}- [glass clinks]
- [liquid pours]

1145
01:00:57.666 --> 01:00:59.226
{\an8}[Benoit humming]

1146
01:00:59.250 --> 01:01:01.333
[intriguing suspenseful music plays]

1147
01:01:04.041 --> 01:01:07.041
{\an8}[Jud] <i>So I've spent the past hour
doing exactly that</i>

1148
01:01:07.541 --> 01:01:10.750
{\an8}<i>and now I'll put down this pen
and hand it to you</i>

1149
01:01:11.250 --> 01:01:12.434
{\an8}<i>and I guess, wait</i>

1150
01:01:12.458 --> 01:01:14.434
{\an8}<i>while you read my story</i>

1151
01:01:14.458 --> 01:01:17.500
<i>of the murder
of Monsignor Jefferson Wicks.</i>

1152
01:01:18.000 --> 01:01:20.000
{\an8}[intriguing suspenseful music continues]

1153
01:01:25.208 --> 01:01:26.250
[sighs softly]

1154
01:01:31.791 --> 01:01:32.791
[sighs]

1155
01:01:34.291 --> 01:01:35.291
[music fades]

1156
01:01:44.083 --> 01:01:45.291
Why'd you do it?

1157
01:01:48.625 --> 01:01:50.125
Better question is,

1158
01:01:50.708 --> 01:01:53.392
why'd I think I could lie to you
and get away with it?

1159
01:01:53.416 --> 01:01:56.125
Oh, no, you didn't lie.
I knew you wouldn't.

1160
01:01:57.000 --> 01:01:59.351
You just didn't say
the dishonest part out loud.

1161
01:01:59.375 --> 01:02:01.166
[suspenseful music plays]

1162
01:02:03.125 --> 01:02:07.601
"When I joined the others outside,
the police were just arriving."

1163
01:02:07.625 --> 01:02:09.892
- [distant sirens wailing]
- [suspenseful music continues]

1164
01:02:09.916 --> 01:02:11.625
<i>"Joined the others outside."</i>

1165
01:02:12.125 --> 01:02:14.101
<i>So you stayed inside.</i>

1166
01:02:14.125 --> 01:02:17.017
<i>So you were the only person
with unobserved access</i>

1167
01:02:17.041 --> 01:02:21.500
<i>to the utility closet after the murder,
but before the police searched it.</i>

1168
01:02:23.541 --> 01:02:25.041
Why? I mean... [scoffs]

1169
01:02:28.000 --> 01:02:29.267
Why protect him?

1170
01:02:29.291 --> 01:02:31.375
I didn't do it to protect Wicks.

1171
01:02:33.041 --> 01:02:35.351
I did it to save the people
who believe in him

1172
01:02:35.375 --> 01:02:36.851
just a little disillusionment.

1173
01:02:36.875 --> 01:02:39.767
Well, surely everyone knew.

1174
01:02:39.791 --> 01:02:43.351
<i>I mean, it would have been
on his breath after every Mass.</i>

1175
01:02:43.375 --> 01:02:46.392
<i>"Fortified himself."
Oh, clever wording there.</i>

1176
01:02:46.416 --> 01:02:48.642
<i>But everyone must have known.</i>

1177
01:02:48.666 --> 01:02:50.875
The spirit really moved him today, huh?

1178
01:02:51.541 --> 01:02:52.958
No, not everyone.

1179
01:02:53.458 --> 01:02:54.541
Sam.

1180
01:02:55.333 --> 01:02:57.517
The one good person in this whole place.

1181
01:02:57.541 --> 01:02:59.625
Getting sober saved his life.

1182
01:03:01.041 --> 01:03:04.875
<i>I knew the press and police were there.
Why let that be part of the story?</i>

1183
01:03:06.458 --> 01:03:07.267
[clangs]

1184
01:03:07.291 --> 01:03:09.767
[Jud] <i>Wicks had been stabbed.</i>

1185
01:03:09.791 --> 01:03:13.601
<i>I don't know how or by who,
but I... but I knew he'd been stabbed.</i>

1186
01:03:13.625 --> 01:03:15.684
<i>So this had nothing to do with it.</i>

1187
01:03:15.708 --> 01:03:17.892
<i>It was an impulse.</i>

1188
01:03:17.916 --> 01:03:20.517
A little storytelling to protect my flock.

1189
01:03:20.541 --> 01:03:22.226
Oh, bullshit!

1190
01:03:22.250 --> 01:03:26.416
And in protecting their bubble of belief,
you have shielded a killer!

1191
01:03:29.000 --> 01:03:30.625
Where is that flask?

1192
01:03:31.916 --> 01:03:34.833
- [objects clattering]
- [Jud] Shit!

1193
01:03:37.458 --> 01:03:39.642
Shit. It's not here. [panting]

1194
01:03:39.666 --> 01:03:40.726
[music fades]

1195
01:03:40.750 --> 01:03:41.916
Blanc.

1196
01:03:42.875 --> 01:03:44.083
I'm sorry.

1197
01:03:44.708 --> 01:03:46.684
He was stabbed, so I didn't think--

1198
01:03:46.708 --> 01:03:48.892
No. You did not think.

1199
01:03:48.916 --> 01:03:51.267
But we're into the woods now,
so you better start.

1200
01:03:51.291 --> 01:03:52.625
[panting]

1201
01:03:53.291 --> 01:03:55.125
Someone broke into my room.

1202
01:03:56.125 --> 01:03:57.416
It's just hitting me.

1203
01:03:57.958 --> 01:04:00.767
This is devious,
it's calculated against me.

1204
01:04:00.791 --> 01:04:03.208
And now you see
the enemy we're up against.

1205
01:04:05.125 --> 01:04:07.976
You have listened to this flock's stories
with empathy and grace,

1206
01:04:08.000 --> 01:04:09.809
but we're done with that now.

1207
01:04:09.833 --> 01:04:11.517
